WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.340
&gt;&gt; Today's talk with

00:00:05.341 --> 00:00:10.020
me by Professor Anish
Arora. Thank you.

00:00:10.021 --> 00:00:10.680
I'll try to give

00:00:10.681 --> 00:00:12.120
an introduction for
Professor Anish Arora.

00:00:12.121 --> 00:00:13.710
I know him through Manik.

00:00:13.711 --> 00:00:16.342
I guess, as we saw
yesterday in past lessons

00:00:16.343 --> 00:00:20.370
in the next work in
wireless sensing,

00:00:20.371 --> 00:00:22.200
I think he's worked
on various networks,

00:00:22.201 --> 00:00:25.245
and sensing networks in the past.

00:00:25.246 --> 00:00:27.525
Right? Past 20 years.

00:00:27.526 --> 00:00:30.600
Professor Anish has been
using Microsoft Research

00:00:30.601 --> 00:00:34.510
for the last 20 years, again.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:38.010
From what I gathered,

00:00:38.011 --> 00:00:40.410
they've spent 10 years

00:00:40.411 --> 00:00:44.070
on building their
hardware ecosystem,

00:00:44.071 --> 00:00:48.790
and datasets around what
he'll be talking today.

00:00:48.791 --> 00:00:52.190
So, I hope we can learn
from their expperience.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:56.450
&gt;&gt; Thank you. Thank you
for the invitation.

00:00:56.451 --> 00:00:58.590
It's great to be
at Microsoft with

00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:00.300
all these wonderful
minds and to meet

00:01:00.301 --> 00:01:02.310
all friends and run into lots

00:01:02.311 --> 00:01:04.905
of new folks and great new ideas.

00:01:04.906 --> 00:01:07.100
By the way, 20 years ago,

00:01:07.101 --> 00:01:10.005
we started working on
device networks at Microsoft.

00:01:10.006 --> 00:01:12.520
So, Bill Gates had
his own home network,

00:01:12.521 --> 00:01:14.280
and different groups in

00:01:14.281 --> 00:01:16.285
Microsoft were coming up
with their own version.

00:01:16.286 --> 00:01:18.685
So, when research,
Human Vangh and I,

00:01:18.686 --> 00:01:20.445
were working on device networks.

00:01:20.446 --> 00:01:22.720
Somehow that attracted
DARPA's attention,

00:01:22.721 --> 00:01:27.530
which got me to working in
wireless sensor networks.

00:01:27.531 --> 00:01:30.160
Before that, I did distributed
computing in theory.

00:01:30.161 --> 00:01:32.380
So, building things
is something I

00:01:32.381 --> 00:01:35.785
credit Microsoft to
getting me into.

00:01:35.786 --> 00:01:39.580
So, yesterday, we spoke about

00:01:39.581 --> 00:01:44.975
the role of Mote-Scale computing,

00:01:44.976 --> 00:01:46.950
and how it leverages
machine learning,

00:01:46.951 --> 00:01:49.165
and it's poised to
leverage deep learning.

00:01:49.166 --> 00:01:51.980
So, today, I really want
to focus on deep learning.

00:01:51.981 --> 00:01:55.120
I'll just pick up on
that topic of issues we

00:01:55.121 --> 00:01:59.020
run into using shallow
machine learning at Mote-Scale.

00:01:59.021 --> 00:02:01.410
These issues I'll
phrase in terms of

00:02:01.411 --> 00:02:03.250
this concept of limited signals,

00:02:03.251 --> 00:02:04.680
which is a vague concept.

00:02:04.681 --> 00:02:06.480
But, I'll try to
give you different

00:02:06.481 --> 00:02:08.575
conceptions of limited signal,

00:02:08.576 --> 00:02:11.475
and how it impacts
the design in question.

00:02:11.476 --> 00:02:13.140
Yeah? So, just as a recap.

00:02:13.141 --> 00:02:15.435
Mote-Scale is
battery-powered stuff.

00:02:15.436 --> 00:02:20.790
You've got radios, your wireless.

00:02:20.791 --> 00:02:23.750
The consumption of
power is limited.

00:02:23.751 --> 00:02:26.775
It's typically well
below 100 milliwatts.

00:02:26.776 --> 00:02:28.470
You could use energy harvesting.

00:02:28.471 --> 00:02:30.060
You have one or more sensors.

00:02:30.061 --> 00:02:31.680
You have one or more processors.

00:02:31.681 --> 00:02:40.860
So, just to illustrate the
details of one type of Mote,

00:02:40.861 --> 00:02:43.095
here is something that
we have been using.

00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:46.185
For a decade or so,
we use systems like

00:02:46.186 --> 00:02:52.860
these small 8-bit Motes
from say TI,

00:02:52.861 --> 00:02:56.320
and used an operating system
called TinyOS,

00:02:56.321 --> 00:02:57.990
which was a remarkable concept

00:02:57.991 --> 00:03:00.110
for being a resource constraint.

00:03:00.111 --> 00:03:01.560
It turned out TinyOS,

00:03:01.561 --> 00:03:03.100
even though we trained
thousands of students,

00:03:03.101 --> 00:03:04.560
didn't transition very well.

00:03:04.561 --> 00:03:07.380
Quite simply because it
was not easy to program.

00:03:07.381 --> 00:03:11.090
So, here you have the challenge
of addressing low-power,

00:03:11.091 --> 00:03:13.290
and this low-power can exploit

00:03:13.291 --> 00:03:17.230
all these rich low-power modes.

00:03:17.231 --> 00:03:20.415
For example, this
cortex M3 Mote that

00:03:20.416 --> 00:03:24.160
we called the.NOW Mote,

00:03:24.161 --> 00:03:26.955
has really nice low-power states.

00:03:26.956 --> 00:03:29.865
It's really a software
design challenge

00:03:29.866 --> 00:03:32.980
to be able to exercise those
low-power states correctly.

00:03:32.981 --> 00:03:35.880
So, to address
this issue that it was

00:03:35.881 --> 00:03:39.060
hard to get people to
change how they program,

00:03:39.061 --> 00:03:41.580
we had to revert to
a standard programming language.

00:03:41.581 --> 00:03:43.605
We said, we would work in C#.

00:03:43.606 --> 00:03:46.260
Yeah. So, we actually took
a system which was built

00:03:46.261 --> 00:03:48.850
at Microsoft Research
about 20 years ago,

00:03:48.851 --> 00:03:50.950
it's called the.NET
Micro Framework,

00:03:50.951 --> 00:03:53.695
and we took the.NET Micro
Framework, and we stripped it.

00:03:53.696 --> 00:03:55.260
We made it near real time,

00:03:55.261 --> 00:03:56.890
improved its jitter,

00:03:56.891 --> 00:03:59.415
and added wireless
capabilities to it.

00:03:59.416 --> 00:04:01.530
So, what we've ended up with

00:04:01.531 --> 00:04:04.030
is a runtime that
we call the eMote,

00:04:04.031 --> 00:04:06.925
where you can run at
the moment interpreted,

00:04:06.926 --> 00:04:10.545
and not just in time
compiled C# programs,

00:04:10.546 --> 00:04:13.005
and we get pretty decent jitter.

00:04:13.006 --> 00:04:16.560
We can program application
specific networks.

00:04:16.561 --> 00:04:18.510
The way we do this is by

00:04:18.511 --> 00:04:22.350
abstracting the radio link
as a virtual link.

00:04:22.351 --> 00:04:23.960
These could be multiple radios,

00:04:23.961 --> 00:04:25.950
so all links are offered to

00:04:25.951 --> 00:04:29.275
the network pattern
as a virtual link.

00:04:29.276 --> 00:04:31.050
So, you can custom design

00:04:31.051 --> 00:04:34.465
your network at
an abstract level.

00:04:34.466 --> 00:04:37.710
With this framework
for virtualization,

00:04:37.711 --> 00:04:41.465
and these very general
network patterns,

00:04:41.466 --> 00:04:43.950
we can come up with a rich class

00:04:43.951 --> 00:04:46.740
of wireless sensor networks.

00:04:46.741 --> 00:04:49.025
The point I wanted to make was

00:04:49.026 --> 00:04:51.560
that a Mote is
largely software designed.

00:04:51.561 --> 00:04:53.615
In fact, even the sensors

00:04:53.616 --> 00:04:55.965
are becoming largely
software designed,

00:04:55.966 --> 00:04:57.740
and of course programming

00:04:57.741 --> 00:05:00.440
the application at
this high level is a convenience

00:05:00.441 --> 00:05:05.150
that we would like
to build on. Yes.

00:05:05.151 --> 00:05:07.040
&gt;&gt; What do you mean by sensors
are software designed?

00:05:07.041 --> 00:05:11.630
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, as

00:05:11.631 --> 00:05:14.290
you noticed I showed you
yesterday the radar.

00:05:14.291 --> 00:05:19.835
The radar is actually just
giving a simple complex value.

00:05:19.836 --> 00:05:24.955
But, this complex
value represents

00:05:24.956 --> 00:05:31.355
the cumulative returns
in some region.

00:05:31.356 --> 00:05:33.845
Now, how do I
control that region?

00:05:33.846 --> 00:05:36.850
Should I actually introduce
those knobs in hardware,

00:05:36.851 --> 00:05:39.435
and exercise some kind
of hardware tuning,

00:05:39.436 --> 00:05:42.420
or I can program
those knobs in software?

00:05:42.421 --> 00:05:45.280
So, this particular
radar has an FPGA,

00:05:45.281 --> 00:05:50.335
and most of the sensor control
is in the software itself.

00:05:50.336 --> 00:05:52.570
Increasingly, you
will find that that's

00:05:52.571 --> 00:05:54.800
the right pattern for
leveraging the sensors.

00:05:54.801 --> 00:05:58.800
Right? This is somewhat
counter-intuitive,

00:05:58.801 --> 00:06:00.275
but we keep thinking of

00:06:00.276 --> 00:06:03.780
digital mems as
encoding in hardware,

00:06:03.781 --> 00:06:06.130
more and more
complex computation.

00:06:06.131 --> 00:06:08.270
But, in fact, sometimes
just beginning

00:06:08.271 --> 00:06:10.540
with a much lower power
analog mems,

00:06:10.541 --> 00:06:14.350
and doing the rest in software
is not only more flexible,

00:06:14.351 --> 00:06:15.610
but also lower power.

00:06:15.611 --> 00:06:17.930
So, software defined sensing is

00:06:17.931 --> 00:06:20.719
the thing that we can build on,

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.115
although it continues to
grow at the Mote scale.

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:27.200
It isn't the dominant pattern,

00:06:27.201 --> 00:06:28.460
but what we're finding is it's

00:06:28.461 --> 00:06:31.780
a very useful pattern,
at Mote scale.

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:36.860
Of course, all the features
that you generate,

00:06:36.861 --> 00:06:39.050
as all of us are doing,
is done in software.

00:06:39.051 --> 00:06:40.870
Right? So, we are not
assuming that we're

00:06:40.871 --> 00:06:42.760
limiting ourselves to
some features, which are,

00:06:42.761 --> 00:06:45.430
for example, features
like extracting

00:06:45.431 --> 00:06:48.190
certain filters which are
defined in the hardware itself.

00:06:48.191 --> 00:06:51.205
We are defining our own features.

00:06:51.206 --> 00:06:53.290
So, by limited signal,

00:06:53.291 --> 00:06:56.990
what I mean is various things.

00:06:56.991 --> 00:06:58.900
So, one of the forms
of limited signal

00:06:58.901 --> 00:07:02.615
is I've got a signal
which is rather weak.

00:07:02.616 --> 00:07:05.800
So, we're looking at targets

00:07:05.801 --> 00:07:08.410
which may not be
conceptually large.

00:07:08.411 --> 00:07:10.630
We're in this low-power regime

00:07:10.631 --> 00:07:12.490
where this target compared

00:07:12.491 --> 00:07:13.840
to the whole scene
is rather small.

00:07:13.841 --> 00:07:15.295
So, the signal in terms of

00:07:15.296 --> 00:07:17.790
power itself can be quite small.

00:07:17.791 --> 00:07:20.270
Now, of course, if you've
got an active sensor,

00:07:20.271 --> 00:07:22.350
and the active sensor
is battery powered,

00:07:22.351 --> 00:07:24.410
it's radiating at very low power.

00:07:24.411 --> 00:07:27.000
For example, in
the case of the radar,

00:07:27.001 --> 00:07:28.420
we are radiating energy,

00:07:28.421 --> 00:07:30.120
and the reflections
are coming back in

00:07:30.121 --> 00:07:32.010
a width of fourth power
of reduction.

00:07:32.011 --> 00:07:33.780
So, you've got signal which is

00:07:33.781 --> 00:07:36.030
quite low in the
presence of noise and

00:07:36.031 --> 00:07:37.590
the key design challenges to

00:07:37.591 --> 00:07:40.650
find how you can represent
that signal in a way

00:07:40.651 --> 00:07:43.110
that you get a good quality of

00:07:43.111 --> 00:07:46.680
SNR to still be able to
do your computing tasks.

00:07:46.681 --> 00:07:51.430
Now, in addition to
the noise being large,

00:07:51.431 --> 00:07:54.870
the background
environment presents

00:07:54.871 --> 00:07:59.015
a lot of diversity and
magnitude of return.

00:07:59.016 --> 00:08:01.615
We can think of that as clutter.

00:08:01.616 --> 00:08:04.150
We can place our sensors in

00:08:04.151 --> 00:08:06.930
regimes where in
addition to the target,

00:08:06.931 --> 00:08:08.250
there's a large amount
of clutter,

00:08:08.251 --> 00:08:11.720
which is contributing to
essentially a form of noise,

00:08:11.721 --> 00:08:15.350
which we have to reject. Now,

00:08:15.351 --> 00:08:18.805
sometimes, one can make
a distinction between

00:08:18.806 --> 00:08:21.270
the objects in the scene as being

00:08:21.271 --> 00:08:23.925
targets of interest and
targets not of interests.

00:08:23.926 --> 00:08:25.450
So, there might be a distinction

00:08:25.451 --> 00:08:27.130
between clutter
and targets not of

00:08:27.131 --> 00:08:29.070
interests and
these targets not of

00:08:29.071 --> 00:08:31.190
interest also have
to be rejected.

00:08:31.191 --> 00:08:35.180
Yeah. So, that also
constraints your signal.

00:08:35.181 --> 00:08:37.860
Now, if you get into

00:08:37.861 --> 00:08:41.325
sort of a more interesting
set of concerns,

00:08:41.326 --> 00:08:46.390
this is when sensors can
interfere with each other.

00:08:46.391 --> 00:08:49.645
So, the returns of an object

00:08:49.646 --> 00:08:52.800
can essentially be interfering.

00:08:52.801 --> 00:08:54.945
Now, that can happen
in multiple ways,

00:08:54.946 --> 00:08:56.630
which I'll try and
outline in the talk.

00:08:56.631 --> 00:08:58.430
But we'll have to think about how

00:08:58.431 --> 00:09:01.530
interference affects your design.

00:09:01.531 --> 00:09:03.420
Now, in certain cases,

00:09:03.421 --> 00:09:06.115
you might think about
this interference as being

00:09:06.116 --> 00:09:09.955
somewhat constructively
or adversarially done.

00:09:09.956 --> 00:09:11.585
Somewhat deliberately done.

00:09:11.586 --> 00:09:13.300
So, I'll define
this concept called

00:09:13.301 --> 00:09:15.980
obfuscation and now the question

00:09:15.981 --> 00:09:17.170
becomes, how do you deal with?

00:09:17.171 --> 00:09:18.710
How do you leverage obfuscation?

00:09:18.711 --> 00:09:20.430
Is it a good thing
or a bad thing?

00:09:20.431 --> 00:09:21.680
Of course depends on

00:09:21.681 --> 00:09:24.510
your application and what
can you do about that.

00:09:24.511 --> 00:09:28.640
A form of obfuscation can
simply be spoofing targets,

00:09:28.641 --> 00:09:30.900
by constructively
creating fake targets on

00:09:30.901 --> 00:09:34.095
the scene rather than
just jamming the sensor.

00:09:34.096 --> 00:09:36.950
So, that's a very rough
definition of the kinds

00:09:36.951 --> 00:09:39.460
of challenges we run
into in applications,

00:09:39.461 --> 00:09:41.655
and I wanted to spell out
how we've thought about it.

00:09:41.656 --> 00:09:43.460
Really much of our work

00:09:43.461 --> 00:09:46.760
is driven by the application
and methods are

00:09:46.761 --> 00:09:49.260
quite basic in some cases and
there's a lot of room for

00:09:49.261 --> 00:09:50.470
growing this that could

00:09:50.471 --> 00:09:52.090
lead to an interesting
conversation.

00:09:52.091 --> 00:09:57.600
So, what applications
is sort of incorrect,

00:09:57.601 --> 00:10:01.010
I'll just focus on one
application given the time today.

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:04.830
So, the story about
why we've gotten to

00:10:04.831 --> 00:10:07.595
forest protection is
really quite basic.

00:10:07.596 --> 00:10:09.700
We've in the last 40 years,

00:10:09.701 --> 00:10:12.980
the wildlife of the world
has gone down by 70 percent.

00:10:12.981 --> 00:10:14.880
One way of reacting,

00:10:14.881 --> 00:10:19.140
and this says it's not just sort

00:10:19.141 --> 00:10:23.780
of a simple ecological concern,

00:10:23.781 --> 00:10:26.385
it is also an economic concern.

00:10:26.386 --> 00:10:29.760
There are many dimensions
to this concern about how

00:10:29.761 --> 00:10:31.770
human development is being

00:10:31.771 --> 00:10:34.270
affected by wildlife reduction,

00:10:34.271 --> 00:10:36.360
and one way of reacting to

00:10:36.361 --> 00:10:38.895
that problem is to deal
with large mammals.

00:10:38.896 --> 00:10:40.650
Large mammals at the top of

00:10:40.651 --> 00:10:44.170
the animal species hierarchy have

00:10:44.171 --> 00:10:49.500
this not only this attention
that gets to people,

00:10:49.501 --> 00:10:53.180
but in fact if you're able
to tame the damage to them,

00:10:53.181 --> 00:10:55.135
its contribution to the rest

00:10:55.136 --> 00:10:58.725
of the chain is
quite significant.

00:10:58.726 --> 00:11:02.935
So, we ran into problems
like in India in 2009,

00:11:02.936 --> 00:11:05.200
the tiger went extinct
in certain parks.

00:11:05.201 --> 00:11:07.405
Took them a few years to
discover it was extinct.

00:11:07.406 --> 00:11:08.580
They were looking at pockmarks

00:11:08.581 --> 00:11:09.745
and they thought it was there,

00:11:09.746 --> 00:11:11.280
but in fact they
were over-counting

00:11:11.281 --> 00:11:13.750
the same mark as being
multiple tigers.

00:11:13.751 --> 00:11:15.850
So, they didn't realize
the decline and they

00:11:15.851 --> 00:11:18.410
didn't come up as
being ready for it.

00:11:18.411 --> 00:11:21.645
So, they had to do things
like transplanting tigers.

00:11:21.646 --> 00:11:23.730
So, around 2011, we started

00:11:23.731 --> 00:11:26.120
this project with
a number of partners.

00:11:26.121 --> 00:11:28.944
Where I was looking at
radar sensor networks,

00:11:28.945 --> 00:11:30.680
my colleagues at
ISC we're looking

00:11:30.681 --> 00:11:33.485
at PIR sensor networks.

00:11:33.486 --> 00:11:36.100
My colleagues at Tripolitania
Lehenga we're looking at

00:11:36.101 --> 00:11:37.649
a very cool fiber-optic

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:40.260
based classification
of animal targets.

00:11:40.261 --> 00:11:43.540
Really really good topic,
but that's for another day.

00:11:43.541 --> 00:11:48.600
So, the way this is a baby
formulation of the problem.

00:11:48.601 --> 00:11:54.625
So, you've got a reserve
or a national park,

00:11:54.626 --> 00:11:58.410
and in this park what we
would like to do is to see

00:11:58.411 --> 00:12:03.110
whether people or animals
are at points of interest.

00:12:03.111 --> 00:12:06.330
So, there are crossing points
where either animals should

00:12:06.331 --> 00:12:07.720
not be or people should

00:12:07.721 --> 00:12:09.640
not be and at
these points of interest,

00:12:09.641 --> 00:12:11.965
you want to detect the activity.

00:12:11.966 --> 00:12:15.425
In particular this activity

00:12:15.426 --> 00:12:18.395
has in the sense of
targets not of interests,

00:12:18.396 --> 00:12:21.750
has got to reject some types
of activity while

00:12:21.751 --> 00:12:25.389
focusing on other relevant
types of activity.

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:28.400
So, for example in
the particular, so,

00:12:28.401 --> 00:12:29.570
we did one deployment in

00:12:29.571 --> 00:12:31.060
the Panna National
Reserve which is

00:12:31.061 --> 00:12:32.780
a tiger Reserve then we did

00:12:32.781 --> 00:12:35.835
another deployment in 2016

00:12:35.836 --> 00:12:40.200
in an unnamed park
in South Africa.

00:12:40.201 --> 00:12:42.730
This was done with
the World Wildlife Fund,

00:12:42.731 --> 00:12:46.040
and the idea was here
the poachers when they come in,

00:12:46.041 --> 00:12:47.425
they come in with dogs,

00:12:47.426 --> 00:12:49.700
and they come in small groups
of two or three people.

00:12:49.701 --> 00:12:51.855
So, you had to discriminate
these poachers,

00:12:51.856 --> 00:12:53.360
but when cattle
were going through

00:12:53.361 --> 00:12:55.420
the scene or goats were
going through the scene,

00:12:55.421 --> 00:12:58.635
you didn't have to
alarm the guides.

00:12:58.636 --> 00:13:01.905
Now, it turns out there
is no to my knowledge,

00:13:01.906 --> 00:13:03.860
a national park or
a forest which is

00:13:03.861 --> 00:13:07.310
uniformly covered by
communications, like cellular.

00:13:07.311 --> 00:13:09.350
It turns out you're lucky if at

00:13:09.351 --> 00:13:10.540
the guard stations you got

00:13:10.541 --> 00:13:13.235
cellular or some network access,

00:13:13.236 --> 00:13:15.385
and so we leveraged that.

00:13:15.386 --> 00:13:17.845
Let's assume the guard
station is connected,

00:13:17.846 --> 00:13:19.590
we have these operational points,

00:13:19.591 --> 00:13:20.660
we want to do

00:13:20.661 --> 00:13:23.045
this discrimination at
the operational points,

00:13:23.046 --> 00:13:24.310
reach the guard station,

00:13:24.311 --> 00:13:26.255
from the guard station
reach the cloud.

00:13:26.256 --> 00:13:28.650
From the cloud, contact
the management,

00:13:28.651 --> 00:13:30.750
because guards have
to be kept honest,

00:13:30.751 --> 00:13:35.915
and also if allowed contact
various social networks.

00:13:35.916 --> 00:13:37.740
Because a good way of socializing

00:13:37.741 --> 00:13:40.935
the response to crime
is to actually,

00:13:40.936 --> 00:13:43.130
good way to deal with
crimes to actually

00:13:43.131 --> 00:13:45.280
socialize the response to them,

00:13:45.281 --> 00:13:47.960
depending on how progressive
park managements are.

00:13:47.961 --> 00:13:49.710
Now, for this system to work,

00:13:49.711 --> 00:13:52.200
of course it had to work
in this hostile terrain.

00:13:52.201 --> 00:13:53.695
You've got a low power network

00:13:53.696 --> 00:13:55.290
which has to cover large areas.

00:13:55.291 --> 00:13:58.140
These parks tend to be
hundreds of square kilometers.

00:13:58.141 --> 00:13:59.725
But now it's about 650.

00:13:59.726 --> 00:14:00.930
This park we were in was

00:14:00.931 --> 00:14:04.530
probably about
two-thirds of that.

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:09.050
Then you had to
make sure that this

00:14:09.051 --> 00:14:11.845
was easy to operate
and to maintain.

00:14:11.846 --> 00:14:13.080
That's a key requirement.

00:14:13.081 --> 00:14:14.620
Because even though
you train the guards,

00:14:14.621 --> 00:14:18.375
the guards themselves have
limited technical competence.

00:14:18.376 --> 00:14:21.050
Cost is an important
constraint in the design,

00:14:21.051 --> 00:14:24.735
because these parks are
very cash strapped.

00:14:24.736 --> 00:14:29.450
To some extent the adversary
is very intelligent

00:14:29.451 --> 00:14:34.150
and it's very organized and
it's very legally supported.

00:14:34.151 --> 00:14:36.050
So, you have to be concerned

00:14:36.051 --> 00:14:38.235
about this had been
somewhat stealthy,

00:14:38.236 --> 00:14:40.355
accurate, robust, and reliable.

00:14:40.356 --> 00:14:42.340
So, all these are
issues and that takes

00:14:42.341 --> 00:14:45.070
us as a motivation into
the limited signal case.

00:14:45.071 --> 00:14:46.905
So, here's how we
dealt with this.

00:14:46.906 --> 00:14:50.015
We used this concept
of a virtual fence.

00:14:50.016 --> 00:14:52.650
Although these parks
are very large,

00:14:52.651 --> 00:14:54.280
often the crossings are

00:14:54.281 --> 00:14:57.180
happening in
small linear sections.

00:14:57.181 --> 00:15:00.500
So, for example, in
this park in South Africa,

00:15:00.501 --> 00:15:04.360
there was a remote corner
of the park where you had

00:15:04.361 --> 00:15:05.830
a river which was very easily

00:15:05.831 --> 00:15:09.300
flooded at certain times
of the month,

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:12.890
and there were
certain flood points.

00:15:12.891 --> 00:15:14.590
So, there were
many strips which were

00:15:14.591 --> 00:15:16.985
roughly 100 to 150 meters long.

00:15:16.986 --> 00:15:20.035
So, if you could cover
these 13 strips,

00:15:20.036 --> 00:15:22.510
you had covered one major zone.

00:15:22.511 --> 00:15:27.255
Sometimes you have
a bunch of watering holes

00:15:27.256 --> 00:15:32.585
and poisoning is a form
of getting to the animal,

00:15:32.586 --> 00:15:34.400
so you had to detect what was

00:15:34.401 --> 00:15:37.075
going on at the watering hole.

00:15:37.076 --> 00:15:41.700
Alternatively, a third
pattern was that,

00:15:41.701 --> 00:15:43.440
there were these ingress routes

00:15:43.441 --> 00:15:46.140
where people would
actually drive in.

00:15:46.141 --> 00:15:48.880
Coming fast, shoot and head out.

00:15:48.881 --> 00:15:50.330
Yeah. This is a form of

00:15:50.331 --> 00:15:52.650
crime which is particularly
true in India.

00:15:52.651 --> 00:15:54.780
Whereas, the poachers
in South Africa,

00:15:54.781 --> 00:15:56.780
they were actually
coming on foot and

00:15:56.781 --> 00:15:59.410
walking very fast for
significant distances. Yes?

00:15:59.411 --> 00:16:00.330
&gt;&gt; How did you get to know this?

00:16:00.331 --> 00:16:01.065
&gt;&gt; Say again.

00:16:01.066 --> 00:16:03.680
&gt;&gt; How did you get to know this?

00:16:03.681 --> 00:16:06.710
&gt;&gt; All these things are deeply
well understood, right?

00:16:06.711 --> 00:16:08.110
These patterns are well-known.

00:16:08.111 --> 00:16:10.970
In fact, the organization
is such that,

00:16:10.971 --> 00:16:12.800
sometimes the
villagers are helping

00:16:12.801 --> 00:16:16.960
the poachers because of
complex social relationships.

00:16:16.961 --> 00:16:19.340
You can harm me, I
will not tell on you.

00:16:19.341 --> 00:16:20.900
But when you're coming,
I'll actually feed

00:16:20.901 --> 00:16:22.820
you for the night, let
you sleep and go on.

00:16:22.821 --> 00:16:24.610
These patterns are
well understood,

00:16:24.611 --> 00:16:30.620
but it's distrust between
authorities and local people.

00:16:30.621 --> 00:16:36.140
That's one major source
of contention here.

00:16:36.390 --> 00:16:39.585
The other thing is,

00:16:39.586 --> 00:16:41.840
I'm not suggesting technology

00:16:41.841 --> 00:16:43.310
is the answer to
this big problem.

00:16:43.311 --> 00:16:44.840
It's a very small element

00:16:44.841 --> 00:16:46.675
at the bottom of
the response hierarchy,

00:16:46.676 --> 00:16:48.075
but it's a necessary element,

00:16:48.076 --> 00:16:50.620
and that's the part
that we're focused on.

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:54.030
So, here is just to get

00:16:54.031 --> 00:16:56.525
you to visualize what's
happening in South Africa.

00:16:56.526 --> 00:16:59.000
So, it's about, I would say

00:16:59.001 --> 00:17:02.875
five or six kilometers
from this river area,

00:17:02.876 --> 00:17:04.800
is a guard station.

00:17:04.801 --> 00:17:06.710
The guard station is
on the top of a hill.

00:17:06.711 --> 00:17:08.010
It has this tall chimney.

00:17:08.011 --> 00:17:10.555
On a tall chimney, we've
got a base station going.

00:17:10.556 --> 00:17:13.650
This base station has
got line-of-sight

00:17:13.651 --> 00:17:16.210
visibility to a bunch
of abutting hills.

00:17:16.211 --> 00:17:18.340
These hills could be two
kilometers, three kilometers,

00:17:18.341 --> 00:17:19.970
four kilometers away, so we can

00:17:19.971 --> 00:17:22.335
create single low-power
links to them.

00:17:22.336 --> 00:17:24.915
These routers to which

00:17:24.916 --> 00:17:29.214
the first DARPA links are
could be put on poles,

00:17:29.215 --> 00:17:30.990
they could be put on trees.

00:17:30.991 --> 00:17:33.940
And now at these tops
of these hills,

00:17:33.941 --> 00:17:36.475
we somehow have to go
down into the valley,

00:17:36.476 --> 00:17:38.505
so that we can communicate with

00:17:38.506 --> 00:17:41.135
the sensors which are
placed along the river.

00:17:41.136 --> 00:17:45.345
The sensors are, here scale
size is not an issue.

00:17:45.346 --> 00:17:47.310
We didn't really have
to go much smaller.

00:17:47.311 --> 00:17:50.020
In fact, perhaps in
our earlier deployments,

00:17:50.021 --> 00:17:52.015
we even had this size.

00:17:52.016 --> 00:17:55.559
So, you can mount these
on little branches,

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:59.320
on little trees, and you put
them at a certain height.

00:17:59.321 --> 00:18:00.790
You don't want the radar

00:18:00.791 --> 00:18:02.670
in this case to be
at ground level,

00:18:02.671 --> 00:18:05.420
but here, we had

00:18:05.421 --> 00:18:08.925
a solution which was roughly
on this form factor.

00:18:08.926 --> 00:18:13.955
Now, a little bit of a background
around how radars work.

00:18:13.956 --> 00:18:17.425
So essentially, this is
a micropower Doppler radar.

00:18:17.426 --> 00:18:19.090
So it's not a continuous wave,

00:18:19.091 --> 00:18:20.810
you're shouting out pulses.

00:18:20.811 --> 00:18:23.280
Pulses are sent at
a high frequency.

00:18:23.281 --> 00:18:25.260
You can send them a million,

00:18:25.261 --> 00:18:27.340
or two, or three million
times a second.

00:18:27.341 --> 00:18:29.820
That's the pulse
repetition frequency.

00:18:29.821 --> 00:18:31.600
So this generated, this is

00:18:31.601 --> 00:18:33.550
a very old concept

00:18:33.551 --> 00:18:35.860
of radar which is called
homodyne invader.

00:18:35.861 --> 00:18:40.290
That when these multiple pulses,

00:18:40.291 --> 00:18:43.225
this is an example of
a single pulse is shouted,

00:18:43.226 --> 00:18:44.780
it travels to the target,

00:18:44.781 --> 00:18:46.020
the reflection comes back,

00:18:46.021 --> 00:18:49.065
and the reflection is convolved

00:18:49.066 --> 00:18:53.385
with a duplicate of
the transmitted pulse.

00:18:53.386 --> 00:18:55.940
That convolution

00:18:55.941 --> 00:18:58.765
contains the information
that you're looking for.

00:18:58.766 --> 00:19:02.175
So, we are running at what might

00:19:02.176 --> 00:19:06.460
be a few percent of duty cycle
of a continuous wave,

00:19:06.461 --> 00:19:09.490
and you're shouting out
something that's very low power,

00:19:09.491 --> 00:19:13.060
so the overall power consumption
is not too much.

00:19:13.061 --> 00:19:14.770
That said, this is

00:19:14.771 --> 00:19:17.190
the biggest consumer of
power in our system.

00:19:17.191 --> 00:19:20.415
Over the years, we've had
many versions of the radar.

00:19:20.416 --> 00:19:22.295
We started off with 30 milliwatt,

00:19:22.296 --> 00:19:23.570
went to 40 milliwatt,

00:19:23.571 --> 00:19:25.510
and finally have been
able to pull that

00:19:25.511 --> 00:19:27.770
back to around 16 milliwatt.

00:19:27.771 --> 00:19:29.240
And we think we have
designs which will

00:19:29.241 --> 00:19:31.020
go down to a couple of milliwatt,

00:19:31.021 --> 00:19:33.005
but that's going to
be real research.

00:19:33.006 --> 00:19:36.785
So, you have
this big power consumer,

00:19:36.786 --> 00:19:38.835
but still in
the low-power regime,

00:19:38.836 --> 00:19:43.395
which we are going to analyze
our information from.

00:19:43.396 --> 00:19:45.995
Here, I spoke to this
a little bit yesterday,

00:19:45.996 --> 00:19:49.130
but what you have is
this complex return.

00:19:49.131 --> 00:19:51.000
And this complex return,

00:19:51.001 --> 00:19:53.225
as the target approaches

00:19:53.226 --> 00:19:56.435
has this particular pattern
which grows stronger,

00:19:56.436 --> 00:19:59.185
and the phase rotates,

00:19:59.186 --> 00:20:01.300
and the rotation of the phase

00:20:01.301 --> 00:20:03.740
gives you a sense of how
much distance is traveled.

00:20:03.741 --> 00:20:07.460
Yeah. So, this by

00:20:07.461 --> 00:20:09.915
itself is an interesting
feature to begin with.

00:20:09.916 --> 00:20:13.654
So, if you look at the nature
of the clutter response,

00:20:13.655 --> 00:20:15.070
the clutter response which has

00:20:15.071 --> 00:20:16.510
all these bushes and trees around

00:20:16.511 --> 00:20:18.000
the radar are actually

00:20:18.001 --> 00:20:20.140
not producing much
change in phase.

00:20:20.141 --> 00:20:21.830
This phase is self-canceling.

00:20:21.831 --> 00:20:24.310
But if a human or

00:20:24.311 --> 00:20:26.765
a target not of interest
goes through the scene,

00:20:26.766 --> 00:20:28.625
that is a displacing object.

00:20:28.626 --> 00:20:30.635
Something that's
not moving in situ,

00:20:30.636 --> 00:20:31.910
and you can see that there is

00:20:31.911 --> 00:20:35.730
a significant change in the
phase which we can track.

00:20:35.731 --> 00:20:37.720
So, you've got a basis for

00:20:37.721 --> 00:20:40.230
starting on
discriminating objects.

00:20:40.231 --> 00:20:42.945
But, if you want to
get finer grain,

00:20:42.946 --> 00:20:44.700
then you can look at

00:20:44.701 --> 00:20:48.285
not just the amplitude
and the phas signatures,

00:20:48.286 --> 00:20:51.160
you can look at
the time-frequency signatures.

00:20:51.161 --> 00:20:54.160
So, here are signatures
of how a car,

00:20:54.161 --> 00:20:55.490
which is a rigid object,

00:20:55.491 --> 00:20:58.765
corresponds to a very narrow
band of excited frequencies.

00:20:58.766 --> 00:21:00.910
But the human and a dog,

00:21:00.911 --> 00:21:02.370
which is a soft target,

00:21:02.371 --> 00:21:05.520
have this much richer
frequency spread.

00:21:05.521 --> 00:21:07.730
And within this frequency spread,

00:21:07.731 --> 00:21:11.020
although not very nicely
visible at this resolution,

00:21:11.021 --> 00:21:13.540
are very interesting
micro Doppler buttons.

00:21:13.541 --> 00:21:15.410
You can see not just the torso,

00:21:15.411 --> 00:21:16.950
you can see the limbs.

00:21:16.951 --> 00:21:19.950
You can actually discriminate
the arms and the legs.

00:21:19.951 --> 00:21:21.420
And in fact, that becomes

00:21:21.421 --> 00:21:22.880
a basis from discriminating from

00:21:22.881 --> 00:21:26.865
the dog because the feet
typically are much more spindly,

00:21:26.866 --> 00:21:30.180
and therefore you don't have
much return from the legs.

00:21:30.181 --> 00:21:33.240
&gt;&gt; Is this a spectogram
of the face shift?

00:21:33.241 --> 00:21:35.580
You look at the face shift with

00:21:35.581 --> 00:21:37.315
time, but this is another one.

00:21:37.316 --> 00:21:39.040
&gt;&gt; So, this is frequency.

00:21:39.041 --> 00:21:41.700
What we're seeing is
the frequencies which

00:21:41.701 --> 00:21:44.415
are excited over time, right?

00:21:44.416 --> 00:21:45.945
&gt;&gt; This is the Doppler Shift.

00:21:45.946 --> 00:21:48.820
&gt;&gt; This is the Doppler
shift, precisely.

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:54.650
Now, how do you classify?

00:21:54.651 --> 00:21:56.260
I will not spend
much time on this.

00:21:56.261 --> 00:21:58.040
You track the displacement,

00:21:58.041 --> 00:21:59.850
and you can reject
things which are

00:21:59.851 --> 00:22:01.660
moving in situ based on

00:22:01.661 --> 00:22:03.770
that previously explained idea of

00:22:03.771 --> 00:22:06.545
how the displacement on graphs,

00:22:06.546 --> 00:22:11.805
whereas in situ movements do
not traverse much distance.

00:22:11.806 --> 00:22:14.890
And then, you run
this support vector machine

00:22:14.891 --> 00:22:16.820
which leverages a bunch

00:22:16.821 --> 00:22:18.430
of features that
we have designed.

00:22:18.431 --> 00:22:22.340
It took a while to layout
something like 80 features.

00:22:22.341 --> 00:22:26.135
Here is an example of
a feature which says that,

00:22:26.136 --> 00:22:28.090
what is the area of

00:22:28.091 --> 00:22:32.925
excitation above a certain
threshold in the spectrogram?

00:22:32.926 --> 00:22:36.220
So for a dog, that anomaly area

00:22:36.221 --> 00:22:38.490
is much smaller than
for a human target,

00:22:38.491 --> 00:22:40.750
and we hope this has
an interesting feature.

00:22:40.751 --> 00:22:42.215
We have no idea whether to work.

00:22:42.216 --> 00:22:43.640
We'd throw data at it,

00:22:43.641 --> 00:22:46.250
and we look at the relevance
of these features.

00:22:46.251 --> 00:22:47.845
How much information
gain they have,

00:22:47.846 --> 00:22:50.130
and we tried to select features.

00:22:50.131 --> 00:22:52.470
Then, we of course
implement this,

00:22:52.471 --> 00:22:55.665
and details of that are not
relevant at the moment.

00:22:55.666 --> 00:22:58.555
Any questions before
I really begin?

00:22:58.556 --> 00:23:01.910
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] do we
calibrate the [inaudible].

00:23:01.911 --> 00:23:03.360
&gt;&gt; So, this is the story
I would like to

00:23:03.361 --> 00:23:04.975
tell in the next few slides.

00:23:04.976 --> 00:23:07.885
The aspiration is, I will collect

00:23:07.886 --> 00:23:12.835
data from multiple clutters,
multiple backgrounds,

00:23:12.836 --> 00:23:14.890
on multiple targets, on

00:23:14.891 --> 00:23:17.890
multiple instances of radar
of different generations,

00:23:17.891 --> 00:23:21.070
and come up with a classifier
that works for all.

00:23:21.071 --> 00:23:22.595
That's the aspiration.

00:23:22.596 --> 00:23:24.390
And the story I'd like to tell as

00:23:24.391 --> 00:23:26.360
well which parts we succeeded on,

00:23:26.361 --> 00:23:28.795
which bytes are still moving.

00:23:28.796 --> 00:23:31.420
But, the brief answer is
for the longest time,

00:23:31.421 --> 00:23:35.315
we've stuck with one classifier
for all environments.

00:23:35.316 --> 00:23:37.835
That's the thing we're
working towards.

00:23:37.836 --> 00:23:44.170
Okay. So, let's begin by saying,

00:23:44.171 --> 00:23:46.340
"Well, clutters are
very different."

00:23:46.341 --> 00:23:51.430
So, here is the response
in terms of amplitude.

00:23:51.431 --> 00:23:53.350
Sorry, this is hard to read.

00:23:53.351 --> 00:23:54.890
Amplitude of grass,

00:23:54.891 --> 00:23:57.500
amplitude in a more
idealized setting,

00:23:57.501 --> 00:23:59.180
where one would imagine
there are no reflectors

00:23:59.181 --> 00:24:00.825
like just tarmac.

00:24:00.826 --> 00:24:04.810
This is an airport where we
do experiments for DARPA.

00:24:04.811 --> 00:24:08.260
Here is the change
in phase over time,

00:24:08.261 --> 00:24:10.760
that access is called
rotations per second.

00:24:10.761 --> 00:24:12.760
So, the unwrapped number of

00:24:12.761 --> 00:24:14.950
rotations per second gives

00:24:14.951 --> 00:24:16.740
you a sense of how
phase is changing.

00:24:16.741 --> 00:24:18.960
As you can see, these are

00:24:18.961 --> 00:24:20.330
significantly different in

00:24:20.331 --> 00:24:22.260
both the amplitude
and the phase domain.

00:24:22.261 --> 00:24:25.250
I don't know if it's absolutely
visible, but they are.

00:24:25.251 --> 00:24:27.950
Yeah. So, now the target returns

00:24:27.951 --> 00:24:30.850
will be superposed on
these variable clutter returns.

00:24:30.851 --> 00:24:33.910
And the thinking is,

00:24:33.911 --> 00:24:35.600
it's possible if you've collected

00:24:35.601 --> 00:24:37.680
enough diverse environments to

00:24:37.681 --> 00:24:41.010
estimate a worst case
background clutter,

00:24:41.011 --> 00:24:44.140
and to choose a
displacement threshold

00:24:44.141 --> 00:24:47.245
for targets in
these background clutters,

00:24:47.246 --> 00:24:50.820
which will lead to a constant
number of false alarms,

00:24:50.821 --> 00:24:53.795
so the false alarm
rate will be constant.

00:24:53.796 --> 00:24:55.720
So, we want to allow
something like one a

00:24:55.721 --> 00:24:57.775
month or one a year,

00:24:57.776 --> 00:25:03.015
and that at this point of
description is feasible.

00:25:03.016 --> 00:25:06.440
So, I'll give a chart
to that momentarily.

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:09.190
That's a simple explanation.

00:25:09.191 --> 00:25:11.060
The more interesting
explanation is,

00:25:11.061 --> 00:25:14.155
you would have thought
that grass is,

00:25:14.156 --> 00:25:16.050
there's more clutter in

00:25:16.051 --> 00:25:18.290
grass than in
the idealized concrete.

00:25:18.291 --> 00:25:21.835
So you should have had
higher noise in the grass case.

00:25:21.836 --> 00:25:23.385
Turns out it's the opposite.

00:25:23.386 --> 00:25:24.780
A lot of that has to do with

00:25:24.781 --> 00:25:26.460
the implementation of the radar,

00:25:26.461 --> 00:25:27.610
the particular choice of

00:25:27.611 --> 00:25:29.430
the components and
how the intermodes,

00:25:29.431 --> 00:25:31.975
et cetera, get affected
by these targets.

00:25:31.976 --> 00:25:33.720
In fact, concrete is

00:25:33.721 --> 00:25:37.665
an environment in which
the phase noise drifts.

00:25:37.666 --> 00:25:39.090
Whereas, when there
is more clutter,

00:25:39.091 --> 00:25:42.415
the phase noise actually
tends to become quiescent,

00:25:42.416 --> 00:25:46.875
counter-intuitive, but
again something unanticipated.

00:25:46.876 --> 00:25:49.630
Now, if we just step back from

00:25:49.631 --> 00:25:52.040
clutter and just look at
the underlying board noise.

00:25:52.041 --> 00:25:53.775
The underlying
board noise itself,

00:25:53.776 --> 00:25:56.890
has varied significantly
across various generations.

00:25:56.891 --> 00:25:59.045
This is a very recent radar,

00:25:59.046 --> 00:26:01.500
the 16 milliwatt radar
I was referring to,

00:26:01.501 --> 00:26:04.740
where the median of
the amplitude in this,

00:26:04.741 --> 00:26:06.859
is just plain simple noise,

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:09.600
is this number 10.

00:26:09.601 --> 00:26:13.830
Whereas, the radar that
we put in South Africa,

00:26:13.831 --> 00:26:18.000
the median of that is says
substantially higher at 72.4.

00:26:18.001 --> 00:26:21.790
Yeah. And conversely, when
you look at the rotations,

00:26:21.791 --> 00:26:25.220
you'll see that
the noise rotations

00:26:25.221 --> 00:26:27.300
are higher for
this radar as well.

00:26:27.301 --> 00:26:29.850
For different radars,
we have to be

00:26:29.851 --> 00:26:32.160
able to tolerate
this underlying noise.

00:26:32.161 --> 00:26:34.230
The way we do that is by

00:26:34.231 --> 00:26:37.730
doing an online estimation
of the background noise.

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:41.970
You maintain this estimate of

00:26:41.971 --> 00:26:44.725
the background amplitude
and what you say is,

00:26:44.726 --> 00:26:48.460
"I'm going to choose some number
that I have learned,

00:26:48.461 --> 00:26:52.330
some estimate,
some functional estimate of

00:26:52.331 --> 00:26:55.865
the background noise which if
it shows up in the return,

00:26:55.866 --> 00:26:57.220
I will treat that as noise and I

00:26:57.221 --> 00:26:59.415
will reject that sample."

00:26:59.416 --> 00:27:01.090
I'm actually going
to be throwing away

00:27:01.091 --> 00:27:02.925
samples which corresponds to what

00:27:02.926 --> 00:27:06.825
I am hypothesizing
is a noise return.

00:27:06.826 --> 00:27:09.530
Now, the net effect
of that is that of

00:27:09.531 --> 00:27:11.840
course you're actually throwing
away some signal returns.

00:27:11.841 --> 00:27:13.370
You've got soft targets.

00:27:13.371 --> 00:27:15.695
The soft target
amplitudes will vary.

00:27:15.696 --> 00:27:18.740
Some of those soft target
returns we will throw away.

00:27:18.741 --> 00:27:21.740
We are going to underestimate
our displacement,

00:27:21.741 --> 00:27:23.800
and so the learning of

00:27:23.801 --> 00:27:25.590
the displacement threshold now

00:27:25.591 --> 00:27:28.345
gets coupled with the sample
rejection threshold.

00:27:28.346 --> 00:27:30.560
Around 2010, what we found

00:27:30.561 --> 00:27:33.425
was that in many environments if,

00:27:33.426 --> 00:27:35.775
this is like an empty field.

00:27:35.776 --> 00:27:37.230
There's a parking garage.

00:27:37.231 --> 00:27:40.985
There are lots of trees,
grasses, different places,

00:27:40.986 --> 00:27:43.285
and we found that
a single threshold

00:27:43.286 --> 00:27:45.790
could give you
this constant false alarm.

00:27:45.791 --> 00:27:47.650
But as we went to generations

00:27:47.651 --> 00:27:50.120
of the radar where
the noise grew a lot,

00:27:50.121 --> 00:27:53.385
that gave us a
displacement threshold

00:27:53.386 --> 00:27:55.770
where we started to
have false negatives.

00:27:55.771 --> 00:27:57.840
We had to introduce this concept

00:27:57.841 --> 00:27:59.490
of background rejection and

00:27:59.491 --> 00:28:01.270
after we do background
rejection in all

00:28:01.271 --> 00:28:03.600
of the environments
we've collected data on,

00:28:03.601 --> 00:28:06.895
that technique will work.

00:28:06.896 --> 00:28:09.390
We've come up with
this online algorithm

00:28:09.391 --> 00:28:12.085
for estimating the noise

00:28:12.086 --> 00:28:18.245
and having trained for
various levels of noise,

00:28:18.246 --> 00:28:20.005
for various levels of rejection,

00:28:20.006 --> 00:28:22.450
what the threshold
is going to be.

00:28:22.451 --> 00:28:24.890
&gt;&gt; What's applied there?

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:28.590
&gt;&gt; Here, what we have is

00:28:28.591 --> 00:28:30.660
a log scale which says
the probability of

00:28:30.661 --> 00:28:33.035
the false alarm as a function

00:28:33.036 --> 00:28:37.235
of the distance
that is traversed.

00:28:37.236 --> 00:28:42.025
Yeah. What this is saying is,

00:28:42.026 --> 00:28:46.565
if you look at a case
where you have trees,

00:28:46.566 --> 00:28:51.830
the probability of the
false alarm drops more quickly.

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:56.785
In order to reject tree movement,

00:28:56.786 --> 00:29:02.960
a threshold of 0.17 meters
would have been sufficient to

00:29:02.961 --> 00:29:05.490
give you a probability
of false alarm which

00:29:05.491 --> 00:29:08.980
was significantly less than
one false alarm a week,

00:29:08.981 --> 00:29:10.780
or one false alarm a month.

00:29:10.781 --> 00:29:13.820
Yeah. But you'd have to
use a larger threshold to

00:29:13.821 --> 00:29:19.670
reject whatever
this parking garage one is.

00:29:19.671 --> 00:29:22.745
As the noise increased for us,

00:29:22.746 --> 00:29:24.730
this threshold that we had to

00:29:24.731 --> 00:29:27.670
reject became something
like 0.65 meters,

00:29:27.671 --> 00:29:29.420
and that was competing with what

00:29:29.421 --> 00:29:31.610
actual displacements
on targets were.

00:29:31.611 --> 00:29:34.165
So we would have gotten
false negatives.

00:29:34.166 --> 00:29:37.755
Yeah. In order to reign
in the false negative,

00:29:37.756 --> 00:29:40.140
we introduced the background
rejection and then

00:29:40.141 --> 00:29:43.040
chose this universal
threshold backed here.

00:29:43.041 --> 00:29:44.830
What we are saying is
something like 0.4

00:29:44.831 --> 00:29:46.530
meters is sufficient to

00:29:46.531 --> 00:29:50.280
reject clutter in
these different environments.

00:29:50.281 --> 00:29:51.280
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:29:50.282 --> 00:29:56.970
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:29:56.971 --> 00:29:59.340
This is the phase shift.

00:29:59.341 --> 00:30:03.040
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:30:03.041 --> 00:30:06.075
&gt;&gt; Yeah, in rotations yes, yes.

00:30:06.076 --> 00:30:09.220
In a window. Exactly, precisely.

00:30:09.221 --> 00:30:12.925
Yeah. So the window of
course is dictated by

00:30:12.926 --> 00:30:14.750
the windows that we use

00:30:14.751 --> 00:30:18.105
for discriminating
displacement, right?

00:30:18.106 --> 00:30:21.130
There are also, we've got
to build in the fact that

00:30:21.131 --> 00:30:23.980
our targets are not moving
continuously, they pause.

00:30:23.981 --> 00:30:26.005
Yeah. They will
change orientation,

00:30:26.006 --> 00:30:27.720
so we have to introduce
some techniques

00:30:27.721 --> 00:30:30.900
for handling those
cases in those Windows.

00:30:30.901 --> 00:30:34.285
In fact, I suspect those ideas
may be relevant for you.

00:30:34.286 --> 00:30:35.815
We can take that offline.

00:30:35.816 --> 00:30:37.460
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:30:38.310 --> 00:30:39.715
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] also.

00:30:39.716 --> 00:30:43.070
&gt;&gt; Yes. This data set
includes high wind,

00:30:43.071 --> 00:30:44.910
presence of rain, moving

00:30:44.911 --> 00:30:48.730
water nearby in
the scene et cetera.

00:30:48.731 --> 00:30:50.640
This is painful, right? We have

00:30:50.641 --> 00:30:52.430
to collect all these data.

00:30:52.431 --> 00:30:57.385
Now, to get to Shusa's question.

00:30:57.386 --> 00:31:01.140
The question is how do you
design a classifier which

00:31:01.141 --> 00:31:03.270
works in a new environment

00:31:03.271 --> 00:31:05.170
which you haven't
encountered before?

00:31:05.171 --> 00:31:07.465
The very simple observation
is trying not to

00:31:07.466 --> 00:31:10.170
train from data collected
in similar environments.

00:31:10.171 --> 00:31:11.800
There is a real gap between

00:31:11.801 --> 00:31:14.075
the performance that
you expect to get,

00:31:14.076 --> 00:31:17.420
between a system which
has been trained for

00:31:17.421 --> 00:31:19.580
different environments
versus a system

00:31:19.581 --> 00:31:22.335
which has been trained
in similar environments.

00:31:22.336 --> 00:31:27.640
We started doing designs
where we are looking at

00:31:27.641 --> 00:31:34.540
what the cross-environmental
information gain features.

00:31:34.610 --> 00:31:37.935
That leads to
this very simple idea

00:31:37.936 --> 00:31:39.110
but maybe I'll spell it out.

00:31:39.111 --> 00:31:41.595
Here is a feature
which is not robust.

00:31:41.596 --> 00:31:43.545
If you look at frequency noise

00:31:43.546 --> 00:31:45.340
and you look at the volume of

00:31:45.341 --> 00:31:48.440
frequency of the energy

00:31:48.441 --> 00:31:50.920
in this spectral domain
above a certain threshold,

00:31:50.921 --> 00:31:53.110
that can change
quite substantially.

00:31:53.111 --> 00:31:55.650
So it's not a robust feature.

00:31:55.651 --> 00:31:58.930
But, if you just project

00:31:58.931 --> 00:32:08.580
that spectral power of

00:32:08.581 --> 00:32:11.420
activation above a threshold into

00:32:11.421 --> 00:32:15.245
the area or the shape
of that activation,

00:32:15.246 --> 00:32:17.840
then that seems to
be much more robust.

00:32:17.841 --> 00:32:21.530
We'd like to find in
our feature selection,

00:32:21.531 --> 00:32:24.110
not just features which
have high information gain,

00:32:24.111 --> 00:32:25.890
but which are likely to show

00:32:25.891 --> 00:32:29.570
very low variance across
different environments.

00:32:29.571 --> 00:32:31.690
That led to this work where we

00:32:31.691 --> 00:32:34.060
came up with techniques for

00:32:34.061 --> 00:32:36.560
starting off with things like

00:32:36.561 --> 00:32:39.915
MRMR information gain
or information gain,

00:32:39.916 --> 00:32:42.700
but then actually pruning
out those features by adding

00:32:42.701 --> 00:32:45.580
this dispersion
component which we

00:32:45.581 --> 00:32:49.045
called the Mean
Absolute Deviation.

00:32:49.046 --> 00:32:52.230
We're trying to basically

00:32:52.231 --> 00:32:55.185
minimize this variance and
that's modeled by trying

00:32:55.186 --> 00:32:58.560
this hyperparameter
which takes into account

00:32:58.561 --> 00:33:00.275
the relative importance of

00:33:00.276 --> 00:33:03.095
the information gain
and the variability.

00:33:03.096 --> 00:33:06.140
Roughly speaking,
the very simple idea

00:33:06.141 --> 00:33:08.780
is choose those
features which are

00:33:08.781 --> 00:33:10.750
going to have low variance across

00:33:10.751 --> 00:33:14.890
environments while not
losing information too much.

00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:19.560
That's the answer
to your question.

00:33:19.561 --> 00:33:22.490
Now, we run into cases
which are more interesting.

00:33:22.491 --> 00:33:27.270
Okay. The first case
is a case where,

00:33:27.700 --> 00:33:33.010
assume that the pulses generated
by a radar are periodic.

00:33:33.011 --> 00:33:36.460
Assume accidentally
the pulses generated by

00:33:36.461 --> 00:33:40.210
two radar are perfectly aligned.

00:33:40.211 --> 00:33:43.290
Now the return of a target from

00:33:43.291 --> 00:33:46.525
radar one might show up in

00:33:46.526 --> 00:33:53.415
the reference pulses which
are convolved for radar two.

00:33:53.416 --> 00:33:55.900
Yeah. Now targets which are

00:33:55.901 --> 00:33:58.865
potentially not even in
the scene of radar one,

00:33:58.866 --> 00:34:01.320
sorry, not in the scene
of radar two are showing

00:34:01.321 --> 00:34:03.690
up because radar one
is exciting them.

00:34:03.691 --> 00:34:09.425
Yeah. That's a problem that
shouldn't be allowed, right?

00:34:09.426 --> 00:34:10.760
Normally, we don't really place

00:34:10.761 --> 00:34:12.400
radars very close to each other.

00:34:12.401 --> 00:34:14.480
If I'm counting the number
of people in this room,

00:34:14.481 --> 00:34:16.150
one radar should be sufficient,

00:34:16.151 --> 00:34:18.580
but you know if there's
a neighboring room

00:34:18.581 --> 00:34:20.845
which has its own radar
the question is,

00:34:20.846 --> 00:34:24.375
am I seeing the targets
there in my return here?

00:34:24.376 --> 00:34:27.040
The way you typically handle

00:34:27.041 --> 00:34:29.930
this is by desynchronizing
the pulses,

00:34:29.931 --> 00:34:33.385
and that's where software
control becomes helpful.

00:34:33.386 --> 00:34:36.180
Yeah. How much randomness
you want to create,

00:34:36.181 --> 00:34:37.420
what patterns you want to create

00:34:37.421 --> 00:34:39.795
become something
that you program.

00:34:39.796 --> 00:34:43.695
Now, another topic is,

00:34:43.696 --> 00:34:46.540
it's one thing to
say a single target

00:34:46.541 --> 00:34:48.080
displaced for the scene
and the phase

00:34:48.081 --> 00:34:49.540
unwrapped nicely but what

00:34:49.541 --> 00:34:51.990
happens if multiple targets
are in the scene?

00:34:51.991 --> 00:34:54.190
The simple cases is
when one target is

00:34:54.191 --> 00:34:56.160
somehow dominant,
it's close to you.

00:34:56.161 --> 00:34:59.045
Essentially, what you are
tracking is that one target.

00:34:59.046 --> 00:35:01.540
But if there are cases
where you want to

00:35:01.541 --> 00:35:05.265
count and the other target is
behind the dominant target,

00:35:05.266 --> 00:35:07.980
how do you discover that?

00:35:07.981 --> 00:35:11.420
So, we did some work on
counting classifiers,

00:35:11.421 --> 00:35:13.745
actually regression
functions for counting.

00:35:13.746 --> 00:35:18.510
Well, the very, very high level
summary is, first of all,

00:35:18.511 --> 00:35:20.560
the longer you observe,

00:35:20.561 --> 00:35:21.705
the more likely

00:35:21.706 --> 00:35:25.000
the obfuscated targets
will reveal themselves.

00:35:25.001 --> 00:35:26.800
Counter-intuitive, but it took

00:35:26.801 --> 00:35:28.880
us a little while
to discover that.

00:35:28.881 --> 00:35:30.770
The second one was that there are

00:35:30.771 --> 00:35:32.390
certain features where you can

00:35:32.391 --> 00:35:36.000
look at shadows of objects,

00:35:36.001 --> 00:35:37.780
hidden imprints of objects.

00:35:37.781 --> 00:35:39.450
So, we discovered
the need for looking in

00:35:39.451 --> 00:35:41.775
the spectrogram for gradients.

00:35:41.776 --> 00:35:43.870
So, you had this strong return,

00:35:43.871 --> 00:35:45.380
this area of excitation.

00:35:45.381 --> 00:35:50.235
But you also saw that
there was a smooth,

00:35:50.236 --> 00:35:52.510
not too sharp
gradient besides it,

00:35:52.511 --> 00:35:54.315
which was indicative
of some other

00:35:54.316 --> 00:35:56.530
objects which were there.

00:35:56.531 --> 00:35:58.800
So, you started using
those gradient features to

00:35:58.801 --> 00:36:01.405
build your estimates of
the number of people.

00:36:01.406 --> 00:36:07.650
So, any questions on that idea?

00:36:07.651 --> 00:36:09.600
Really, these are
separate pieces of work,

00:36:09.601 --> 00:36:12.120
and I'm not explaining
them at much length.

00:36:12.121 --> 00:36:13.205
&gt;&gt; Sir.

00:36:13.206 --> 00:36:13.885
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:36:13.886 --> 00:36:15.725
&gt;&gt; So, in the case
of cross-target,

00:36:15.726 --> 00:36:18.065
do you want to develop

00:36:18.066 --> 00:36:19.390
on the target or you just want

00:36:19.391 --> 00:36:21.070
to set above the one target?

00:36:21.071 --> 00:36:24.650
&gt;&gt; Yes. It depends
on your application.

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:28.820
So, in the sense that
I was describing

00:36:28.821 --> 00:36:30.200
for this room that's really about

00:36:30.201 --> 00:36:32.050
counting the utilization
of the room.

00:36:32.051 --> 00:36:33.560
So, we can do various things.

00:36:33.561 --> 00:36:35.510
We could decide how much air

00:36:35.511 --> 00:36:38.255
to pump through a room
based on its utilization.

00:36:38.256 --> 00:36:41.210
That's an energy saving. So, yes.

00:36:41.211 --> 00:36:42.510
Sometimes, you really do want to

00:36:42.511 --> 00:36:45.374
count the number of targets.

00:36:45.375 --> 00:36:47.850
Another example is

00:36:47.851 --> 00:36:51.870
this bike pedestrian network
that we are deploying,

00:36:51.871 --> 00:36:56.205
where we want multiple points

00:36:56.206 --> 00:37:00.310
of utilization across
a Trail Network.

00:37:00.311 --> 00:37:02.455
Currently, that's not
very well handled.

00:37:02.456 --> 00:37:05.590
What you have is
data from phones,

00:37:05.591 --> 00:37:07.560
where people who are going

00:37:07.561 --> 00:37:09.860
through those trails
are self-reporting.

00:37:09.861 --> 00:37:12.060
But you don't know what fraction
of the population is

00:37:12.061 --> 00:37:15.220
doing that very well to
build your estimates.

00:37:15.221 --> 00:37:18.315
The other technologies
are quite manual.

00:37:18.316 --> 00:37:21.390
So, the planning in a city
like Columbus is largely

00:37:21.391 --> 00:37:24.300
based on trying out
some technologies,

00:37:24.301 --> 00:37:26.980
but actually depending
on human counts than

00:37:26.981 --> 00:37:30.260
twice a year to estimate
which trails are being used,

00:37:30.261 --> 00:37:31.770
which ones to fix etcetera.

00:37:31.771 --> 00:37:35.330
So, we'd like to actually do low

00:37:35.331 --> 00:37:39.870
cost counting at multiple points
in the Trail Network.

00:37:40.270 --> 00:37:46.550
Here, this problem of
obfuscation is basic for us.

00:37:48.070 --> 00:37:50.970
Now, there is a different form of

00:37:50.971 --> 00:37:54.475
obfuscation which is
somewhat privacy-centric.

00:37:54.476 --> 00:38:01.370
So here, I'll be describing
radar-like classification,

00:38:01.371 --> 00:38:03.955
but I'll be doing it in
the traditional comms domain.

00:38:03.956 --> 00:38:05.970
So, in the recent several years,

00:38:05.971 --> 00:38:08.075
there's been a bunch
of work where

00:38:08.076 --> 00:38:10.640
people have looked at
wireless signals and you look

00:38:10.641 --> 00:38:14.190
at the side channel of the
wireless signal in some sense

00:38:14.191 --> 00:38:17.550
by extracting the
low-frequency components

00:38:17.551 --> 00:38:18.965
of the signal out of it,

00:38:18.966 --> 00:38:21.960
by integrating
a number of samples.

00:38:21.961 --> 00:38:27.805
So, just like we were using
a basis for recognizing,

00:38:27.806 --> 00:38:30.425
that there was a target
and the scene by a radar,

00:38:30.426 --> 00:38:32.550
you can look at
the WiFi columns which are

00:38:32.551 --> 00:38:35.120
reflecting off this target
to discriminate,

00:38:35.121 --> 00:38:36.585
is there a person in this room

00:38:36.586 --> 00:38:38.475
or how many people are
there in this room?

00:38:38.476 --> 00:38:41.810
Now, supposing I want to
hide the fact that somebody

00:38:41.811 --> 00:38:46.815
outside can observe
outside my house,

00:38:46.816 --> 00:38:50.320
can observe whether
somebody's at home or not.

00:38:50.321 --> 00:38:52.720
How would I do that? So, that's

00:38:52.721 --> 00:38:56.395
this concept which
I call obfuscation.

00:38:56.396 --> 00:38:59.750
So, the idea is
something like this.

00:39:01.060 --> 00:39:04.355
This is the signal
being transmitted.

00:39:04.356 --> 00:39:06.720
This is the signal
being received either

00:39:06.721 --> 00:39:09.865
at the Wi-Fi receiver
or at your sensor.

00:39:09.866 --> 00:39:15.850
That depends, that has
these three degrees of freedom.

00:39:15.851 --> 00:39:18.915
So, the target influences
the amplitude,

00:39:18.916 --> 00:39:21.085
it influences the Doppler shift,

00:39:21.086 --> 00:39:23.160
and it influences
the propagation delay.

00:39:23.161 --> 00:39:25.500
So, I have these three
degrees of freedom,

00:39:25.501 --> 00:39:29.370
and I don't know what sensor
this is going to be used,

00:39:29.371 --> 00:39:31.514
but the sensor is
somehow going to use

00:39:31.515 --> 00:39:33.940
some combination of information

00:39:33.941 --> 00:39:35.220
in these three degrees
of freedom.

00:39:35.221 --> 00:39:38.815
Okay. So, what I would
like to do is to somehow

00:39:38.816 --> 00:39:42.965
obscure the information in
these degrees as follows.

00:39:42.966 --> 00:39:45.625
So, what I'm going to do is to

00:39:45.626 --> 00:39:49.805
introduce yet another node
that I call an obfuscator,

00:39:49.806 --> 00:39:55.020
which is going to transmit
copies of the incoming signal.

00:39:55.021 --> 00:39:57.820
So, this signal is being sent

00:39:57.821 --> 00:40:01.470
in parallel to
the transmission signal.

00:40:01.471 --> 00:40:05.620
So, it all has to be
constructive in some sense.

00:40:05.621 --> 00:40:07.950
So, we are using for
this obfuscated and

00:40:07.951 --> 00:40:12.675
implementation which is
based on Full Duplex radios.

00:40:12.676 --> 00:40:17.355
So, in this copy that
we are going to relay,

00:40:17.356 --> 00:40:19.620
we are going to perturb in
these three dimensions.

00:40:19.621 --> 00:40:20.830
We're going to perturb

00:40:20.831 --> 00:40:23.290
the by adding
an amplitude component,

00:40:23.291 --> 00:40:24.930
we are going to perturb by adding

00:40:24.931 --> 00:40:27.445
a potentially different
Doppler component

00:40:27.446 --> 00:40:30.335
and a different phase component.

00:40:30.336 --> 00:40:35.335
So, the net result is
that instantaneously,

00:40:35.336 --> 00:40:38.995
we can distort the amplitude
and the phase.

00:40:38.996 --> 00:40:44.300
But it turns out that
in the frequency,

00:40:44.301 --> 00:40:46.160
that is still discriminable.

00:40:46.161 --> 00:40:48.215
In order to compensate for that,

00:40:48.216 --> 00:40:50.820
the rate at which your distort
becomes relevant.

00:40:50.821 --> 00:40:53.425
So, as long as you are
distorting at a rate,

00:40:53.426 --> 00:40:58.165
which is faster than what
sensor was trying to use,

00:40:58.166 --> 00:41:01.100
then you should be sufficient.

00:41:01.320 --> 00:41:04.460
There are some very
interesting details about

00:41:04.461 --> 00:41:08.070
how the Full Duplex deals
with self-cancellation,

00:41:08.071 --> 00:41:09.660
which I'm not going
to talk about.

00:41:09.661 --> 00:41:11.065
But the interesting
thing is this,

00:41:11.066 --> 00:41:16.910
an obfuscator can itself
prevent a sensor from working,

00:41:16.911 --> 00:41:20.475
while itself, being
able to sense.

00:41:20.476 --> 00:41:24.130
So, we can create
legitimate sensors while

00:41:24.131 --> 00:41:28.345
simultaneously obfuscating
illegitimate sensors,

00:41:28.346 --> 00:41:30.840
without by the way
affecting communication,

00:41:30.841 --> 00:41:32.690
and that's a whole other story.

00:41:32.691 --> 00:41:35.120
The communication rate in
fact doesn't go down it

00:41:35.121 --> 00:41:37.530
goes up because we are
in fact relaying. Yes?

00:41:37.531 --> 00:41:40.050
&gt;&gt; So can [inaudible] also use
simulitaneous obfuscating?

00:41:40.051 --> 00:41:41.790
&gt;&gt; Yes of course. Yes of course.

00:41:41.791 --> 00:41:44.720
Yeah. So, this is always
a cat and mouse game.

00:41:44.721 --> 00:41:47.320
This research itself
is pure cat and mouse,

00:41:47.321 --> 00:41:51.375
because I'm talking
about a single sensor,

00:41:51.376 --> 00:41:53.195
a single antenna sensor.

00:41:53.196 --> 00:41:58.420
If you got a distributed
array of observers,

00:41:58.421 --> 00:42:00.910
then this technique will
say is not sufficient,

00:42:00.911 --> 00:42:02.650
we have to move to
the next level in

00:42:02.651 --> 00:42:06.095
that game of obfuscation.

00:42:06.096 --> 00:42:09.490
So, here is sort of showing how-

00:42:09.491 --> 00:42:13.465
as you introduce
distortions in copies,

00:42:13.466 --> 00:42:19.220
what happens to the granularity

00:42:19.221 --> 00:42:23.875
of the spectrograms as
the Doppler shift changes?

00:42:23.876 --> 00:42:26.425
So, this higher rate of change

00:42:26.426 --> 00:42:32.000
will give you a result
something like this.

00:42:32.001 --> 00:42:33.920
Let's assume this is

00:42:33.921 --> 00:42:36.225
the pattern that the
sensor was looking for.

00:42:36.226 --> 00:42:39.000
After obfuscation,
you've essentially

00:42:39.001 --> 00:42:41.900
removed the components
in every part of this,

00:42:41.901 --> 00:42:45.035
so that pattern should no
longer be discoverable.

00:42:45.036 --> 00:42:46.560
So, we gave implementations

00:42:46.561 --> 00:42:47.930
PhyCloak and we showed how they

00:42:47.931 --> 00:42:53.930
performed in at least
in the lab setting.

00:42:54.330 --> 00:42:57.460
So, an interesting comment there

00:42:57.461 --> 00:42:59.940
is much of the
technology, actually,

00:42:59.941 --> 00:43:01.815
it's harder to make it robust

00:43:01.816 --> 00:43:04.105
across different
environments for sensing,

00:43:04.106 --> 00:43:06.290
but it's easier to obfuscate it.

00:43:06.291 --> 00:43:08.035
That problem is easier.

00:43:08.036 --> 00:43:10.385
Because all I'm doing is
I'm being destructive,

00:43:10.386 --> 00:43:13.775
in the sensing domain not
the communication domain.

00:43:13.776 --> 00:43:14.760
&gt;&gt; So, it means a [inaudible]

00:43:14.761 --> 00:43:16.680
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:43:16.681 --> 00:43:17.910
This is all RF, right?

00:43:17.911 --> 00:43:19.310
Yeah. In the radar,

00:43:19.311 --> 00:43:22.315
you can actually do
something similar which is,

00:43:22.316 --> 00:43:24.260
you can inject signatures.

00:43:24.261 --> 00:43:26.840
So, if I know what

00:43:26.841 --> 00:43:30.675
signature is to be
expected by your sensor,

00:43:30.676 --> 00:43:32.120
I can pretend that there is

00:43:32.121 --> 00:43:34.590
somebody in the room
by spoofing that.

00:43:34.591 --> 00:43:37.795
My obfuscator can now
become a spoofer.

00:43:37.796 --> 00:43:44.240
This is an example of a
measured spoofing to show how

00:43:44.241 --> 00:43:52.220
you could emulate a particular
gesture like a pull.

00:43:52.221 --> 00:43:54.090
So, the last topic I'd like to

00:43:54.091 --> 00:43:56.935
focus on in terms of
limited signal is,

00:43:56.936 --> 00:43:59.915
how do we deal with
the lack of data?

00:43:59.916 --> 00:44:02.400
This is a problem we all face.

00:44:02.401 --> 00:44:05.210
We go around buildings

00:44:05.211 --> 00:44:08.330
in different ways and
collect information,

00:44:08.331 --> 00:44:10.650
malls, forests, et cetera.

00:44:10.651 --> 00:44:14.905
But there's a lot of
problems in this process.

00:44:14.906 --> 00:44:16.970
Most importantly,
it's inefficient.

00:44:16.971 --> 00:44:19.625
So, the cost of
this process is quite high.

00:44:19.626 --> 00:44:22.740
So, in Chandigarh,
we collected data on

00:44:22.741 --> 00:44:28.535
tigers and bears and elephants.

00:44:28.536 --> 00:44:30.930
This may be [inaudible] ,

00:44:30.931 --> 00:44:33.640
and we didn't get
fantastic data at all.

00:44:33.641 --> 00:44:36.045
Tigers are very lazy
in the afternoon.

00:44:36.046 --> 00:44:39.485
You don't want to collect
data on tigers in zoos.

00:44:39.486 --> 00:44:43.424
So really, for building a tiger
versus human classifier,

00:44:43.425 --> 00:44:47.370
I need data from elsewhere.

00:44:47.450 --> 00:44:52.895
The impact of not having
data is quite simple.

00:44:52.896 --> 00:44:55.220
Here for example,

00:44:55.221 --> 00:44:57.200
you remember the phase chart I

00:44:57.201 --> 00:44:58.650
showed you for
a human and a ball?

00:44:58.651 --> 00:45:02.760
Here is the time-frequency
component for a large ball,

00:45:02.761 --> 00:45:04.410
which is seen close to the radar

00:45:04.411 --> 00:45:08.275
versus a human that's quite
far away from the radar.

00:45:08.276 --> 00:45:10.190
You know from
a pattern perspective,

00:45:10.191 --> 00:45:11.580
they look quite similar.

00:45:11.581 --> 00:45:15.900
So, If I didn't have
sufficient data on weak humans,

00:45:15.901 --> 00:45:19.040
this classifier would
likely had made a mistake.

00:45:19.041 --> 00:45:24.255
Conversely, what we
find is that sometimes,

00:45:24.256 --> 00:45:28.225
a single parameter for
our features is not enough.

00:45:28.226 --> 00:45:31.120
You need multiple parameters
for your feature to

00:45:31.121 --> 00:45:34.000
actually capture
the variability of targets.

00:45:34.001 --> 00:45:35.770
So, to be able to capture

00:45:35.771 --> 00:45:38.190
both the strong ball end
the week ball,

00:45:38.191 --> 00:45:41.295
I might need multiple
parameter instances.

00:45:41.296 --> 00:45:43.155
If I don't have
a rich enough data set,

00:45:43.156 --> 00:45:45.450
I don't capture that fact.

00:45:46.580 --> 00:45:50.770
Now, the problem
is almost wicked,

00:45:50.771 --> 00:45:55.870
because your targets are
quite variable in size.

00:45:55.871 --> 00:45:58.445
They actually work
quite differently,

00:45:58.446 --> 00:46:01.660
men and women and children
walk differently.

00:46:01.661 --> 00:46:04.170
Their speeds tend to
be quite variable,

00:46:04.171 --> 00:46:06.085
their trajectories
are quite unknown.

00:46:06.086 --> 00:46:08.315
They could be stopping, turning.

00:46:08.316 --> 00:46:10.775
Dogs like to just go
around in circles,

00:46:10.776 --> 00:46:12.380
and that's having a good time,

00:46:12.381 --> 00:46:13.840
and their activities
while they're

00:46:13.841 --> 00:46:15.400
doing this can be
changing quite a bit.

00:46:15.401 --> 00:46:16.680
So, you need that variability

00:46:16.681 --> 00:46:18.790
built in into your data collect.

00:46:18.791 --> 00:46:21.120
Now, what sort of worse is,

00:46:21.121 --> 00:46:22.770
the problem is almost inverted

00:46:22.771 --> 00:46:24.500
because we've collected data

00:46:24.501 --> 00:46:26.130
from radars which were placed

00:46:26.131 --> 00:46:28.160
as I was telling you
at about three feet.

00:46:28.161 --> 00:46:31.700
But what if a radar is
placed at eight feet?

00:46:33.630 --> 00:46:37.195
In the vertical dimension,

00:46:37.196 --> 00:46:38.960
it's a 30 degree angle.

00:46:38.961 --> 00:46:40.610
So, the response that
you would get would

00:46:40.611 --> 00:46:43.150
be very elevation dependent.

00:46:43.151 --> 00:46:46.810
The orientation of
the radar matters just as well,

00:46:46.811 --> 00:46:49.030
because it aspires to be

00:46:49.031 --> 00:46:51.920
an omnidirectional sensor
but it is not exactly one.

00:46:51.921 --> 00:46:54.600
It has got strong frontal lobes,

00:46:54.601 --> 00:46:58.025
somewhat strong back lobes
and weaker side lobes.

00:46:58.026 --> 00:47:00.180
So, the gain in different
directions is different.

00:47:00.181 --> 00:47:04.100
As I told you,

00:47:04.101 --> 00:47:05.980
the noise characteristics
can be different,

00:47:05.981 --> 00:47:08.770
and across builds,
the wavelength can be different.

00:47:08.771 --> 00:47:10.270
&gt;&gt; Are you using
an omnidirection item?

00:47:10.271 --> 00:47:10.735
&gt;&gt; Why?

00:47:10.736 --> 00:47:11.380
&gt;&gt; Are you using
an omnidirection?

00:47:11.381 --> 00:47:14.690
&gt;&gt; Yes. Yes. It aspires

00:47:14.691 --> 00:47:17.335
to be an omnidirection
but has artifacts in it.

00:47:17.336 --> 00:47:19.230
So, even the central,

00:47:19.231 --> 00:47:21.750
front lobe is somewhat
left-leaning.

00:47:21.751 --> 00:47:24.355
It has to do with sort of
board capacitance effects

00:47:24.356 --> 00:47:26.630
and all of that.

00:47:26.631 --> 00:47:30.405
How well impedance
matching is done?

00:47:30.406 --> 00:47:33.550
So, what we would
really like to do is to

00:47:33.551 --> 00:47:36.075
augment our data synthetically.

00:47:36.076 --> 00:47:38.820
I'd like to describe
our evolution and

00:47:38.821 --> 00:47:40.200
augmenting data starting from

00:47:40.201 --> 00:47:43.180
something trivial to
something more interesting.

00:47:45.520 --> 00:47:49.880
So, here's a trivial idea
to get the stage set.

00:47:49.881 --> 00:47:53.210
So, the radar is not
exactly omnidirectional.

00:47:53.211 --> 00:47:54.640
So, you've got different gains.

00:47:54.641 --> 00:48:01.890
So, if I have a radar file of
a target going on a radio,

00:48:01.891 --> 00:48:03.930
where I know how

00:48:03.931 --> 00:48:06.500
much its gain is larger
than another radio,

00:48:06.501 --> 00:48:09.370
so this is controlled datas,

00:48:09.371 --> 00:48:12.830
where a human is walking
along a certain radio.

00:48:13.990 --> 00:48:16.825
If I vary the gain,

00:48:16.826 --> 00:48:19.145
I can produce the same behavior

00:48:19.146 --> 00:48:20.875
on a different orientation.

00:48:20.876 --> 00:48:22.635
Sort of a trivial idea.

00:48:22.636 --> 00:48:25.370
A slightly more
interesting is that,

00:48:25.371 --> 00:48:26.830
if I focus on

00:48:26.831 --> 00:48:29.520
the target who's walking
with a uniform speed,

00:48:29.521 --> 00:48:34.145
big if, then I can come
up with a data set,

00:48:34.146 --> 00:48:36.190
which works at
a different speed by

00:48:36.191 --> 00:48:38.585
actually up-sampling
or down-sampling.

00:48:38.586 --> 00:48:40.810
That turns out to
be conservative.

00:48:40.811 --> 00:48:43.925
This one, as we test in training,

00:48:43.926 --> 00:48:48.280
it's slightly less useful
than the previous one.

00:48:48.600 --> 00:48:53.100
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] take the
signature, the radial signature

00:48:53.101 --> 00:48:54.440
[inaudible] certain radius-

00:48:54.441 --> 00:48:54.720
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:48:54.721 --> 00:48:55.760
&gt;&gt; And use this model

00:48:55.761 --> 00:48:57.350
to imagine what it would look

00:48:57.351 --> 00:48:59.330
like if someone walked
and then get sampling.

00:48:59.331 --> 00:49:01.500
&gt;&gt; Precisely. Yes.

00:49:01.960 --> 00:49:03.624
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] scratch.

00:49:03.625 --> 00:49:06.440
&gt;&gt; No. No, no. So, we're
starting off with a data set,

00:49:06.441 --> 00:49:08.770
some of which is controlled
and well-labeled,

00:49:08.771 --> 00:49:13.365
and we're using that data
through these primitive models,

00:49:13.366 --> 00:49:14.840
and they are primitive, trust me,

00:49:14.841 --> 00:49:18.090
to generate other data.

00:49:19.250 --> 00:49:22.620
If you generalize
this idea and you've

00:49:22.621 --> 00:49:26.210
got walks in different
directions, some along radials,

00:49:26.211 --> 00:49:29.495
some along orthogonals,
different radials,

00:49:29.496 --> 00:49:31.799
you can synthesize pieces

00:49:31.800 --> 00:49:33.910
and come up with
arbitrary trajectories.

00:49:33.911 --> 00:49:35.840
So, you can come up
with a richer data set,

00:49:35.841 --> 00:49:40.750
which is what this last one
was trying to spell out.

00:49:40.751 --> 00:49:42.270
In the interest of time,

00:49:42.271 --> 00:49:44.900
I won't spell it out, it's
really very simple physics.

00:49:44.901 --> 00:49:47.530
Actually, the stuff
is not great at all.

00:49:47.531 --> 00:49:49.250
Because a lot of our features

00:49:49.251 --> 00:49:51.035
are looking for micro-Doppler.

00:49:51.036 --> 00:49:52.680
I modeled house how

00:49:52.681 --> 00:49:56.335
the gross body speed would
show up in this other walk,

00:49:56.336 --> 00:49:58.570
but what about the micro-Doppler?
What have I done to it?

00:49:58.571 --> 00:49:59.810
I may have actually distorted

00:49:59.811 --> 00:50:02.860
it in a way that was
counterproductive.

00:50:02.861 --> 00:50:05.615
So, what this has led us to,

00:50:05.616 --> 00:50:10.130
is to rethinking how we
generate our synthetic data.

00:50:10.131 --> 00:50:12.685
So, what we're doing
now is to say,

00:50:12.686 --> 00:50:14.655
we need a source where we have

00:50:14.656 --> 00:50:19.380
well-labeled data
from a co-sensor.

00:50:19.381 --> 00:50:21.250
In this case, what
we are doing is we

00:50:21.251 --> 00:50:23.280
have video which is giving

00:50:23.281 --> 00:50:29.730
us these temporal sequences
of labeled information.

00:50:29.731 --> 00:50:35.265
Now, then what we
would like to do from

00:50:35.266 --> 00:50:39.070
this co-sensor we would

00:50:39.071 --> 00:50:43.460
like to extract from
that the positions,

00:50:43.461 --> 00:50:48.220
the orientations, the sizes
of the various components.

00:50:48.670 --> 00:50:53.180
Then from that, we should be able

00:50:53.181 --> 00:50:57.960
to generate via radar
simulation models traces.

00:50:57.961 --> 00:51:02.635
So, to keep this somewhat
basic to begin with,

00:51:02.636 --> 00:51:05.615
we start off with video,

00:51:05.616 --> 00:51:08.480
from that video we
extract position,

00:51:08.481 --> 00:51:11.855
orientation other information
and then we feed that into.

00:51:11.856 --> 00:51:13.860
Knowing all the components of

00:51:13.861 --> 00:51:14.920
the foreground and

00:51:14.921 --> 00:51:17.070
perhaps some components
of the background,

00:51:17.071 --> 00:51:19.800
we can superpose
all those returns

00:51:19.801 --> 00:51:21.365
and come up with
the Radar return,

00:51:21.366 --> 00:51:23.030
doing something like that and in

00:51:23.031 --> 00:51:26.120
a radar simulation
toolbox, MATLAB.

00:51:26.121 --> 00:51:30.930
Now, that idea itself can be
made a little bit richer,

00:51:30.931 --> 00:51:35.765
by throwing in
various model parameters.

00:51:35.766 --> 00:51:37.690
So here, even the video

00:51:37.691 --> 00:51:39.380
has been captured at
a certain height,

00:51:39.381 --> 00:51:42.205
at a certain orientation
often from a fixed camera,

00:51:42.206 --> 00:51:45.010
but I want to try
various other orientations

00:51:45.011 --> 00:51:46.460
and elevations.

00:51:46.461 --> 00:51:50.590
So, now I can augment
the model that I'm

00:51:50.591 --> 00:51:52.530
using for generating
the Radar data and

00:51:52.531 --> 00:51:55.470
create many more
simulations out of that.

00:51:55.471 --> 00:51:59.630
So, somewhat
pictorially speaking,

00:51:59.631 --> 00:52:03.420
we had two approaches to
collecting video data.

00:52:03.421 --> 00:52:06.225
One is to using
this Motion Capture.

00:52:06.226 --> 00:52:11.210
Motion Capture you actually
get data for humans,

00:52:11.211 --> 00:52:12.520
for dogs, for vehicles.

00:52:12.521 --> 00:52:14.290
This is somewhat
publicly available,

00:52:14.291 --> 00:52:15.995
sometimes you have to pay for it.

00:52:15.996 --> 00:52:18.310
So, this is nicely labelled data,

00:52:18.311 --> 00:52:22.660
where you've got
the body components

00:52:22.661 --> 00:52:26.925
marked in it from which
skeleton data can be extracted.

00:52:26.926 --> 00:52:29.200
Similarly, even with the Kinect

00:52:29.201 --> 00:52:31.260
you can come up
with skeleton data,

00:52:31.261 --> 00:52:33.430
now these joints can

00:52:33.431 --> 00:52:38.070
be treated as the ellipsoids

00:52:38.071 --> 00:52:41.350
and you can estimate
the limb ellipsoids for that,

00:52:41.351 --> 00:52:46.520
and that can be used to
generate your radar simulation.

00:52:46.521 --> 00:52:49.890
Conversely, you can decompose

00:52:49.891 --> 00:52:55.665
the video into Polygons and
for each polygon element,

00:52:55.666 --> 00:52:58.570
you can look at what
its response is going to be

00:52:58.571 --> 00:53:02.645
and superpose those responses
in your radar simulation.

00:53:02.646 --> 00:53:07.490
Now, having generated
all that data,

00:53:07.491 --> 00:53:11.175
you can now try creating

00:53:11.176 --> 00:53:13.680
other model instances
by throwing in

00:53:13.681 --> 00:53:15.080
the parameter variations from

00:53:15.081 --> 00:53:17.270
some set and come up
with a richer set.

00:53:17.271 --> 00:53:20.445
So now, we're at the point
where we are looking at

00:53:20.446 --> 00:53:23.915
the accuracy of
this richer generation,

00:53:23.916 --> 00:53:25.470
it looks quite promising.

00:53:25.471 --> 00:53:27.010
Coming into this workshop,

00:53:27.011 --> 00:53:28.700
we had this debate about
whether we should use

00:53:28.701 --> 00:53:31.375
synthetic data or not.

00:53:31.376 --> 00:53:34.140
We're not using it now
but we think we should

00:53:34.141 --> 00:53:37.650
be using it because it
is accurate enough.

00:53:37.651 --> 00:53:40.500
Okay, so that was the story
I wanted to share

00:53:40.501 --> 00:53:43.560
about limited signals and

00:53:43.561 --> 00:53:46.660
roughly I'd like to
conclude by looking

00:53:46.661 --> 00:53:50.090
ahead on where we
ought to be focusing.

00:53:50.091 --> 00:53:54.110
So, one thing to ask
is we've had these,

00:53:54.111 --> 00:53:56.330
somewhat primitive ways of

00:53:56.331 --> 00:53:58.720
dealing with limited signal
for shallow learning,

00:53:58.721 --> 00:54:01.255
but these issues are
going to be handled,

00:54:01.256 --> 00:54:04.005
must be handled in
the deep learning work.

00:54:04.006 --> 00:54:06.910
So, how does
deep learning provide us

00:54:06.911 --> 00:54:09.660
a good basis for handling
limited signal issues?

00:54:09.661 --> 00:54:11.420
A bunch of questions
there I think

00:54:11.421 --> 00:54:14.090
which should be
interesting research,

00:54:14.091 --> 00:54:16.600
but the other thing
that we have learned

00:54:16.601 --> 00:54:19.595
and this goes back to
Shishir your question.

00:54:19.596 --> 00:54:22.270
Is it the right thing to learn

00:54:22.271 --> 00:54:25.250
a classifier that works
across all backgrounds,

00:54:25.251 --> 00:54:27.800
that works across all locations,

00:54:27.801 --> 00:54:30.900
and works across
all radar technologies?

00:54:30.901 --> 00:54:32.230
We're coming to the point

00:54:32.231 --> 00:54:33.935
where that's not
the right answer.

00:54:33.936 --> 00:54:36.350
So, we'll have to specialize

00:54:36.351 --> 00:54:39.855
the sensor based on its type,

00:54:39.856 --> 00:54:41.600
based on its location and some of

00:54:41.601 --> 00:54:44.425
this is going to involve
online learning.

00:54:44.426 --> 00:54:48.375
So, of course we need
a basis by which sensors can

00:54:48.376 --> 00:54:52.045
share their model drift
with the cloud,

00:54:52.046 --> 00:54:54.700
and the cloud can come up
with new general models for

00:54:54.701 --> 00:54:56.770
everyone or more interestingly

00:54:56.771 --> 00:54:58.415
you can do federated learning.

00:54:58.416 --> 00:55:00.770
So, what we're running
into a cases while

00:55:00.771 --> 00:55:04.475
every sensor is estimating
its own background,

00:55:04.476 --> 00:55:06.915
it needs a basis for
being able to relearn on

00:55:06.916 --> 00:55:08.830
its model and that can be

00:55:08.831 --> 00:55:11.170
done locally and/or
assisted by the cloud.

00:55:11.171 --> 00:55:12.550
So, what are
the patterns for that.

00:55:12.551 --> 00:55:14.160
So those I think are going to

00:55:14.161 --> 00:55:16.590
be very interesting
for limited signals.

00:55:16.591 --> 00:55:19.210
So, with that I'll be
happy to take questions.

00:55:19.211 --> 00:55:28.250
&gt;&gt; So, what sessions

00:55:28.251 --> 00:55:30.425
are thinking of like
the online sessions?

00:55:30.426 --> 00:55:33.735
&gt;&gt; Now, actually
sonic. So, yesterday

00:55:33.736 --> 00:55:37.425
I spoke about this Sounds
of New York City project,

00:55:37.426 --> 00:55:40.510
which I didn't have
time to explain today,

00:55:40.511 --> 00:55:44.445
but the situation there
is you're placing sensors

00:55:44.446 --> 00:55:48.615
in a very different
parts of the city.

00:55:48.616 --> 00:55:50.920
So, some can be an urban canyons,

00:55:50.921 --> 00:55:53.740
some can be in city parks,

00:55:53.741 --> 00:55:57.630
some can be in
housing areas which

00:55:57.631 --> 00:56:01.265
are a mixture of trees
and low buildings.

00:56:01.266 --> 00:56:02.450
So, in all of these the

00:56:02.451 --> 00:56:05.485
propagation characteristics
will be quite different.

00:56:05.486 --> 00:56:09.880
It turns out we do
have information about

00:56:09.881 --> 00:56:12.030
these different parts of
the city which can be used to

00:56:12.031 --> 00:56:14.420
model how sound will propagate.

00:56:14.421 --> 00:56:17.070
So, we have folks on
the data analytic side and

00:56:17.071 --> 00:56:19.845
the visualization side
who can tell us,

00:56:19.846 --> 00:56:23.415
what we think propagation
characteristics would be.

00:56:23.416 --> 00:56:27.975
So, that can be given as
input to your cloud learner,

00:56:27.976 --> 00:56:31.855
but the other question
that we can ask is.

00:56:31.856 --> 00:56:34.780
How can a censor at

00:56:34.781 --> 00:56:39.185
a given location notice that
things have changed enough,

00:56:39.186 --> 00:56:41.350
enough foliage has changed,

00:56:41.351 --> 00:56:43.230
that my model is no longer

00:56:43.231 --> 00:56:46.285
working and become
a basis for triggering?

00:56:46.286 --> 00:56:51.300
Or can a sensor benefit
from sharing

00:56:51.301 --> 00:56:58.240
it's, what's the right word?

00:57:02.090 --> 00:57:04.835
Let's call it Mode.

00:57:04.836 --> 00:57:07.830
I hesitate to use
context in this case.

00:57:07.831 --> 00:57:10.860
So, we could have

00:57:10.861 --> 00:57:13.570
our classification outputs be

00:57:13.571 --> 00:57:15.870
done for different modes and can

00:57:15.871 --> 00:57:19.970
a sensor provide its mode to
its neighbors as a way of

00:57:19.971 --> 00:57:22.020
informing them which might be

00:57:22.021 --> 00:57:24.830
the relevant classifier to use?

00:57:24.831 --> 00:57:28.540
So, we're basically exploring
a number of options

00:57:28.541 --> 00:57:34.595
for handling the drift of
the model of a local sensor,

00:57:34.596 --> 00:57:38.390
and one thing that I'm
trying to do is to

00:57:38.391 --> 00:57:42.220
provide the plumping to
support that learning.

00:57:42.221 --> 00:57:44.720
So, we're reserving
enough energy budget

00:57:44.721 --> 00:57:48.010
for sharing data snippets,

00:57:48.011 --> 00:57:51.195
which are indicative
that my model seemed

00:57:51.196 --> 00:57:54.895
to have low confidence

00:57:54.896 --> 00:57:57.930
in assessing this
particular data item,

00:57:57.931 --> 00:57:59.370
so how does this inform

00:57:59.371 --> 00:58:02.370
the learning of
the regional or global model.

00:58:02.371 --> 00:58:07.965
&gt;&gt; But maybe you can do
the online learning sessions,

00:58:07.966 --> 00:58:11.190
maybe collect data
for a day or 7 days.

00:58:11.191 --> 00:58:11.805
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:58:11.806 --> 00:58:14.340
&gt;&gt; Then learn
a specific model for that.

00:58:14.341 --> 00:58:16.370
&gt;&gt; It's possible,
like I mentioned

00:58:16.371 --> 00:58:18.325
we have 30 years of data,

00:58:18.326 --> 00:58:20.210
so we have data from let's say

00:58:20.211 --> 00:58:23.450
50 to 100 locations which
are telling us over

00:58:23.451 --> 00:58:29.560
two years how the sound
characteristics changed.

00:58:31.740 --> 00:58:34.490
So, assuming that that data gets

00:58:34.491 --> 00:58:37.190
labelled and that
labelling is trustworthy,

00:58:37.191 --> 00:58:39.790
perhaps that can be done.

00:58:40.770 --> 00:58:45.555
Now, how predictive that is
of next year or next spring,

00:58:45.556 --> 00:58:48.380
I don't know and that
takes me down this road

00:58:48.381 --> 00:58:49.780
of actually just being able to

00:58:49.781 --> 00:58:52.050
recognize what mode I mean.

00:58:52.051 --> 00:58:54.450
If I can recognize
what mode I'm in,

00:58:54.451 --> 00:58:56.850
I can see which classifier
to bring in from

00:58:56.851 --> 00:59:02.780
a library of classifies, yes.

00:59:02.781 --> 00:59:05.525
&gt;&gt; So, with regard to
changing environment models,

00:59:05.526 --> 00:59:07.880
since we are a working
in the RF modelling,

00:59:07.881 --> 00:59:09.500
can we use the same technology

00:59:09.501 --> 00:59:10.760
for child estimations
that is used

00:59:10.761 --> 00:59:14.840
by several others or?

00:59:14.841 --> 00:59:23.690
&gt;&gt; Okay, so I'm still trying
to parse your question.

00:59:23.691 --> 00:59:27.800
So, perhaps I can talk
about the difference

00:59:27.801 --> 00:59:31.650
between Cellular Modelling
and Low-Power sensor,

00:59:31.651 --> 00:59:34.010
Low-Power RF Modelling in

00:59:34.011 --> 00:59:37.950
as a way of starting
the conversation.

00:59:37.951 --> 00:59:41.480
So, very good planning
tools exist for how

00:59:41.481 --> 00:59:43.370
cellular characteristics
would be across

00:59:43.371 --> 00:59:47.060
the region and they can
tolerate topography quite well,

00:59:47.061 --> 00:59:48.690
and it's probably because

00:59:48.691 --> 00:59:50.710
they're in a
different SNR regime.

00:59:50.711 --> 00:59:53.710
When we tried to use
the similar planning ideas for

00:59:53.711 --> 00:59:57.630
deploying our relays
in South Africa,

00:59:57.631 --> 00:59:59.795
it turned out that we were

00:59:59.796 --> 01:00:02.580
quite bad in estimating
what would work.

01:00:02.581 --> 01:00:04.160
So, let me just illustrate that.

01:00:04.161 --> 01:00:07.440
So in a Low-power Regime
where you can very quickly go

01:00:07.441 --> 01:00:11.140
from constructive to
destructive interference,

01:00:11.141 --> 01:00:14.675
moving a radar by
a three or four feet,

01:00:14.676 --> 01:00:16.450
actually half a wavelength,

01:00:16.451 --> 01:00:20.455
can take you from
constructive to destructive.

01:00:20.456 --> 01:00:23.900
But what we found was
that I had to search in

01:00:23.901 --> 01:00:27.830
that region to find a spot
where I could deploy my radar.

01:00:27.831 --> 01:00:31.210
So, one would have predicted
that going down a hill,

01:00:31.211 --> 01:00:33.290
with this medium amount of

01:00:33.291 --> 01:00:36.315
vegetation should have given
you 300 meters of range,

01:00:36.316 --> 01:00:38.500
but that was not enough
and you had to do

01:00:38.501 --> 01:00:39.890
the search which was extremely

01:00:39.891 --> 01:00:42.010
painful when you're
climbing trees.

01:00:42.011 --> 01:00:44.880
So, as I don't know
how easy it is,

01:00:44.881 --> 01:00:48.590
in this low-power regime
to do what you're saying.

01:00:50.990 --> 01:00:55.930
Yes.

01:00:55.931 --> 01:01:00.185
&gt;&gt; How easy is it for you to
be able to do a camouflage.

01:01:00.186 --> 01:01:03.310
&gt;&gt; Well you know for years
I had my boys trying to

01:01:03.311 --> 01:01:07.080
behave like dogs and we
were collecting data,

01:01:07.580 --> 01:01:11.620
it is not really easy.

01:01:11.660 --> 01:01:14.570
So, I'm going to sort of
distort your question.

01:01:14.571 --> 01:01:16.480
The question is how would
you fall this radar?

01:01:16.481 --> 01:01:18.330
What are the ways of fooling it?

01:01:18.331 --> 01:01:20.500
So, one way of fooling it

01:01:20.501 --> 01:01:22.650
is to actually move
extremely slowly.

01:01:22.651 --> 01:01:25.120
So when you look at
special operations people,

01:01:25.121 --> 01:01:27.000
they can sometimes move a meter

01:01:27.001 --> 01:01:30.410
an hour when they are crossing
very sensitive borders.

01:01:30.411 --> 01:01:34.985
This thing is sensitive up to
2.6 centimeters per second,

01:01:34.986 --> 01:01:37.350
but a meter an hour is much

01:01:37.351 --> 01:01:40.280
slower than 2.6 centimeters,
so your obscured.

01:01:40.281 --> 01:01:44.700
The other thing you can do
is to be extremely fast.

01:01:44.701 --> 01:01:47.690
So, the way the filters
on the radar are set,

01:01:47.691 --> 01:01:49.630
you can pick up this 2.6 meters a

01:01:49.631 --> 01:01:52.195
second and really about
five meters a second.

01:01:52.196 --> 01:01:54.450
But it's possible to run
faster than five meters a

01:01:54.451 --> 01:01:58.620
second as a way of avoiding this.

01:01:58.660 --> 01:02:02.415
We are banking on the fact
that this poacher

01:02:02.416 --> 01:02:06.050
is going through this
densely wooded forest scene,

01:02:06.051 --> 01:02:07.575
through these narrow trails.

01:02:07.576 --> 01:02:10.050
It's actually coming
into this hidden way

01:02:10.051 --> 01:02:12.150
and will get picked up.

01:02:12.151 --> 01:02:13.830
But if you're doing
the same thing with cars,

01:02:13.831 --> 01:02:16.030
this probably will not work.

01:02:21.770 --> 01:02:30.840
Yes.

01:02:30.841 --> 01:02:39.250
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] ?

01:02:39.251 --> 01:02:42.000
&gt;&gt; Yes. It has not
affected us significantly.

01:02:42.001 --> 01:02:45.010
So what I'm reporting are cases

01:02:45.011 --> 01:02:48.080
where we've deployed
sensors far too close,

01:02:48.081 --> 01:02:50.290
where we see a little bit of

01:02:50.291 --> 01:02:53.625
observation of a target even
before it enters the scene.

01:02:53.626 --> 01:02:57.130
That observation is
not very predictable.

01:02:57.270 --> 01:03:00.570
So sometimes, it looks
like a clear target,

01:03:00.571 --> 01:03:02.900
sometimes it doesn't look
like a clear target.

01:03:02.901 --> 01:03:05.340
So, what I'm saying
is that this is

01:03:05.341 --> 01:03:07.760
not a major problem for us in

01:03:07.761 --> 01:03:10.200
practice and it's
a manageable problem

01:03:10.201 --> 01:03:12.760
in part because we
program things.

01:03:12.761 --> 01:03:14.120
As it is, it's

01:03:14.121 --> 01:03:16.370
a very low probability that
the periodic pulses will

01:03:16.371 --> 01:03:20.355
line up and we are also
throwing in randomness.

01:03:20.356 --> 01:03:24.130
Randomness is good because it
makes your radars healthy.

01:03:24.190 --> 01:03:27.800
So, it's harder to see
the pulse pattern.

01:03:27.801 --> 01:03:30.450
You need a higher
order detector to know

01:03:30.451 --> 01:03:34.185
the presence of the
radar. So, yes.

01:03:34.186 --> 01:03:36.860
For us, it hasn't
been a major problem,

01:03:36.861 --> 01:03:42.185
but we are seeing instances
of this in our training data.

01:03:42.186 --> 01:03:43.500
The question is, how

01:03:43.501 --> 01:03:45.020
much is it affecting
our training data

01:03:45.021 --> 01:03:48.080
is what we are trying
to establish right now?

01:03:48.081 --> 01:03:49.960
So, my interest is
I'm seeing if I

01:03:49.961 --> 01:03:53.055
can classify it and prune it,

01:03:53.056 --> 01:03:56.850
so that I'm only learning from
clean data at the moment.

01:04:00.380 --> 01:04:05.300
Yes. [inaudible]

01:04:05.301 --> 01:04:13.920
&gt;&gt; Okay. Good question. So, I

01:04:13.921 --> 01:04:15.190
think the biggest source of

01:04:15.191 --> 01:04:17.620
false alarms has not
been environmental,

01:04:17.621 --> 01:04:20.685
it's been radar design issues.

01:04:20.686 --> 01:04:23.020
So, if you've got a radar,

01:04:23.021 --> 01:04:25.775
which has got
unusually high noise,

01:04:25.776 --> 01:04:28.955
and we haven't gotten
the thresholds right for that,

01:04:28.956 --> 01:04:33.390
that's radar false positives
tend to occur for us.

01:04:33.740 --> 01:04:38.470
But, yes, maybe I should
give another example.

01:04:38.471 --> 01:04:41.860
So, the World Wildlife Fund
tested our system,

01:04:41.861 --> 01:04:45.490
which was doing a human
versus a catalog classifier,

01:04:45.491 --> 01:04:47.290
and it took it onto a horse farm,

01:04:47.291 --> 01:04:49.375
and they tested it with horses.

01:04:49.376 --> 01:04:52.280
They said, "Yes. I'm
seeing humans all the

01:04:52.281 --> 01:04:55.245
time with horses and
your system doesn't work."

01:04:55.246 --> 01:04:58.355
So that, I don't
know how to parse.

01:04:58.356 --> 01:05:00.160
The system wasn't designed to

01:05:00.161 --> 01:05:03.810
discriminate horses as horses.

01:05:03.811 --> 01:05:06.555
So, that has caused
some confusion.

01:05:06.556 --> 01:05:08.140
So, if we're deploying this in

01:05:08.141 --> 01:05:10.990
an environment
where we've trained

01:05:10.991 --> 01:05:13.080
for cattle and goats

01:05:13.081 --> 01:05:15.510
on one side and humans and
dogs on the other side,

01:05:15.511 --> 01:05:18.850
and all of a sudden,
this Writer starts showing up.

01:05:18.851 --> 01:05:20.630
We don't know what's
going to happen.

01:05:20.631 --> 01:05:24.660
So, that can be viewed as a
false positive in the system.

01:05:24.750 --> 01:05:29.500
So, that operator issue
we have today.

01:05:30.380 --> 01:05:38.190
Yes. [inaudible]

01:05:38.191 --> 01:05:41.170
&gt;&gt; Okay. This is not
a ranging radar.

01:05:41.330 --> 01:05:45.840
So, what it lets you do is

01:05:45.841 --> 01:05:47.880
to look at the relative distance

01:05:47.881 --> 01:05:50.275
not the absolute distance.

01:05:50.276 --> 01:05:53.510
Now, if you could do

01:05:53.511 --> 01:05:57.860
very coarse localization
because, for example,

01:05:57.861 --> 01:06:00.240
in partner the way
it was set up was we

01:06:00.241 --> 01:06:02.915
knew there was this trail
coming in close to a village,

01:06:02.916 --> 01:06:04.030
which meant a certain distance,

01:06:04.031 --> 01:06:06.720
so you are actually putting
sensors along the trail.

01:06:06.721 --> 01:06:08.580
So, in that setting, you

01:06:08.581 --> 01:06:10.910
already know where
the nodes are located,

01:06:10.911 --> 01:06:12.250
and you're within a few meters

01:06:12.251 --> 01:06:13.385
of that, and that is sufficient.

01:06:13.386 --> 01:06:15.100
But more importantly, you've got

01:06:15.101 --> 01:06:17.000
actually multiple
sensors observing,

01:06:17.001 --> 01:06:21.740
so you can be more
exact if need be.

01:06:24.440 --> 01:06:27.640
Conversely, there's
a lot of research on

01:06:27.641 --> 01:06:32.040
very accurate localization
with ultra-wideband radar.

01:06:32.041 --> 01:06:34.830
There's a lot of literature
on that if you're interested.

01:06:34.831 --> 01:06:37.480
One of my students, [inaudible]
has written some papers on that.

01:06:37.481 --> 01:06:42.790
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

01:06:42.791 --> 01:06:44.390
&gt;&gt; Yes. You certainly can and it

01:06:44.391 --> 01:06:46.615
costs a lot of power GPS
will take you.

01:06:46.616 --> 01:06:48.870
&gt;&gt; GPS [inaudible]

01:06:48.871 --> 01:06:50.500
&gt;&gt; Yes. Triangulation is

01:06:50.501 --> 01:06:53.055
certainly the way you
handle localization.

01:06:53.056 --> 01:06:55.365
The question is, how do you
get your range estimates?

01:06:55.366 --> 01:06:57.620
So, you can go to
a ranging radar.

01:06:57.621 --> 01:07:00.780
One reason being
introduced the FPGA was so

01:07:00.781 --> 01:07:03.845
that we could create
very narrow range gates.

01:07:03.846 --> 01:07:05.855
So right now, the way
the radar works is

01:07:05.856 --> 01:07:08.530
roughly one and a
half meters from

01:07:08.531 --> 01:07:11.425
the radar to about 12-13 meters,

01:07:11.426 --> 01:07:14.430
is everything there is
treated as one response,

01:07:14.431 --> 01:07:16.170
but you can create range gates,

01:07:16.171 --> 01:07:17.590
which are about a meter long.

01:07:17.591 --> 01:07:19.095
So, now, you can create

01:07:19.096 --> 01:07:20.630
12 such range gates and you can

01:07:20.631 --> 01:07:22.645
sweep the radar to each of them.

01:07:22.646 --> 01:07:25.510
Then, you can localize
within that distance,

01:07:25.511 --> 01:07:29.855
but it's about a half a
meter error rate on average,

01:07:29.856 --> 01:07:31.710
whereas what folks
in localization

01:07:31.711 --> 01:07:32.860
are trying to do to get

01:07:32.861 --> 01:07:35.730
substantive meter and
localization accuracy.

01:07:35.731 --> 01:07:38.085
So, that's a slightly richer.

01:07:38.086 --> 01:07:39.530
That's a harder problem.

01:07:39.531 --> 01:07:45.330
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

01:07:45.331 --> 01:07:47.720
&gt;&gt; This is centered
at 5.8 gigahertz.

01:07:47.721 --> 01:07:50.170
It's a medium bandwidth.

01:07:50.171 --> 01:07:52.785
So, it's about
a 100 megahertz bandwidth.

01:07:52.786 --> 01:07:54.920
It's not an ultra-wideband radar,

01:07:54.921 --> 01:07:56.580
but it's still got
rich information.

01:07:56.581 --> 01:08:13.260
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

01:08:13.261 --> 01:08:14.640
&gt;&gt; That's a good
question. We have

01:08:14.641 --> 01:08:17.280
significant experience with that.

01:08:17.281 --> 01:08:18.960
So, we co-exist with Wi-Fi

01:08:18.961 --> 01:08:20.790
and Bluetooth without
being affected.

01:08:20.791 --> 01:08:22.140
Yup.

01:08:27.800 --> 01:08:33.070
Okay. Very good. Thanks a lot.

